WEBVTT

1
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
AN AGE OF ILLUSORY MAGIC…

2
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:39.067
A person.

3
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:43.333
a fleet.

4
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:47.000
A war.

5
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:51.867
a man.

6
00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:56.467
an idea.

7
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:00.667
a little trick.

8
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:06.000
a ruse to bring down the walls of Troy.

9
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.067
And burn it to the ground amid screams!

10
00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:03.800
What does that mean?

11
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.800
A gift.

12
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.333
About Athens.

13
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:22.600
We have gods in common.

14
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:24.800
Please accept this gift.

15
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:30.533
Save him from the waves!

16
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.267
Take it to the temple of Athena.

17
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:34.400
The war is over.

18
00:02:41.933 --> 00:02:42.933
One person

19
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.733
a fleet

20
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.400
a war

21
00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:55.667
a man…

22
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.200
Stop!

23
00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:02.933
Not that song.

24
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.000
Mommy's little boy!

25
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.200
Run. run. little guy!

26
00:03:12.733 --> 00:03:13.733
Run!

27
00:03:14.867 --> 00:03:17.200
I want to remind them whose fault it is.

28
00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.133
I don't want the song about Odysseus.

29
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.400
I want Odysseus.

30
00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:23.333
So why are you entertaining suitors at his house. then?

31
00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:28.200
In this world. men
do whatever they want.

32
00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:29.533
And as for me—I'll do what I can.

33
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.667
I'm a man now. Mom.

34
00:03:32.733 --> 00:03:34.200
Then kick them out.

35
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.333
Break the Law of Zeus. and you'll see
what they'll do to you if you give them a reason.

36
00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:46.667
I've already lost one husband.

37
00:03:46.733 --> 00:03:48.333
I don't want to lose a son. too.

38
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.600
Dad is coming back.

39
00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:51.333
So where is it?

40
00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.200
Eight years since the fall of Troy.

41
00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.600
It's been almost twenty years since he left us.

42
00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.267
Where is it?

43
00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:11.333
He deserves the same
welcome as all of you.

44
00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.667
Are you calling us beggars?

45
00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:15.600
I'm saying that you've been
welcome here for three years now.

46
00:04:15.667 --> 00:04:18.400
We've been enjoying your
hospitality for so long.

47
00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:21.933
just because your mom doesn't choose.

48
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.667
She's already married.

49
00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:25.067
Odysseus is dead.

50
00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:27.533
What do you know about my father?

51
00:04:29.933 --> 00:04:33.000
That you had wretched livestock and bad wine.

52
00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:35.533
You got the best
during the first two years.

53
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.333
And I know he never came back.

54
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.533
Not yet.

55
00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:43.800
You miss your dad. even though
you never even knew him.

56
00:04:43.867 --> 00:04:46.000
Like a whiny little brat.

57
00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:48.933
Since no one knows
who his father really is.

58
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.600
Don't think you know who mine is.

59
00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:51.933
I. however. knew him.

60
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.600
When we were kids. We used to watch him hunt.

61
00:04:56.733 --> 00:04:59.000
I wanted to set out for Troy.

62
00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:00.467
but he told me to stay.

63
00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:01.933
so I can keep an eye on you.

64
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Me or my mom?

65
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.867
How could anyone not look at her?

66
00:05:06.933 --> 00:05:08.333
My father is coming back.

67
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.133
And he won't be as generous as she is.

68
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.267
So he doesn't follow the Law of Zeus?

69
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:16.267
The law instructs us to treat
strangers the way we would like to be treated ourselves.

70
00:05:16.333 --> 00:05:18.600
because they might be gods in disguise.

71
00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:22.000
And I think it's become clear.
Antinous. that you are not a god.

72
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Well. this one here didn't exactly smell like a god.

73
00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:26.800
That wouldn't be a good disguise.

74
00:05:26.867 --> 00:05:28.533
Be careful.

75
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.867
It might strike you right now.

76
00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.467
That thunderclap was pure luck.

77
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.067
I don't know. You have wise eyes.

78
00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:52.800
The Eyes of Athens. Here.

79
00:05:52.867 --> 00:05:54.733
Are you in cahoots with the merchants?

80
00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.267
From Sparta.

81
00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:58.000
Did Menelaus return from the war?

82
00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.133
Years ago. Didn't Odysseus return?

83
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.600
We don't know if he's alive. Do you know anything?

84
00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:06.467
The story of how he won the war.

85
00:06:06.533 --> 00:06:08.000
We've already heard that. Anything else?

86
00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:09.667
No.

87
00:06:10.333 --> 00:06:14.933
But Sparta is full of
veterans of the Trojan War.

88
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.867
Someone's bound to know something.

89
00:06:16.933 --> 00:06:20.733
Are you Odysseus's son? Go to Sparta.

90
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.200
Go. Go to Menelaus.

91
00:06:23.867 --> 00:06:24.867
He'll welcome you warmly.

92
00:06:26.867 --> 00:06:29.000
The queen wants to speak with the foreigner.

93
00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:32.000
He lives for the news.

94
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.600
No. no? Don't give me false hope.

95
00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:39.800
Somehow. he can tell if someone
has actually seen Odysseus.

96
00:06:39.867 --> 00:06:41.333
Do you see that one?

97
00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.400
He's almost blind. but that doesn't stop him

98
00:06:43.467 --> 00:06:47.800
to beat to death the travelers
who lie about my father.

99
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.933
No. no. That's too fast.

100
00:07:17.533 --> 00:07:18.600
And how do you know that?

101
00:07:18.667 --> 00:07:22.200
You don't need eyes to
sense a sudden maneuver.

102
00:07:22.267 --> 00:07:23.667
Mentor. push him.

103
00:07:31.933 --> 00:07:33.133
No. no. That's way too fast.

104
00:07:42.533 --> 00:07:44.000
He's just as friendly as you are.

105
00:07:44.933 --> 00:07:46.467
You just obeyed your father.

106
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.733
Where did you find him?

107
00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.533
They met right here.

108
00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:56.600
Come.

109
00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.067
I'll take it.

110
00:08:01.133 --> 00:08:02.267
Is that for your son?

111
00:08:02.333 --> 00:08:03.467
Like a hunting dog.

112
00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.600
And he was right.

113
00:08:42.133 --> 00:08:44.933
Don't move. Do you really want a scar that badly?

114
00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.467
Why do you always make the bow ring?

115
00:08:48.533 --> 00:08:49.667
That's fair.

116
00:08:49.733 --> 00:08:51.400
Should you warn the prey?

117
00:08:51.467 --> 00:08:52.533
To fight with honor.

118
00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:54.533
But this isn't a battle. You're hunting.

119
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.533
Hunting is a cautious battle.

120
00:08:57.067 --> 00:09:01.067
Very clever. Yes. But that cleverness
is going to get you into trouble.

121
00:09:01.133 --> 00:09:02.133
Try again.

122
00:09:02.733 --> 00:09:05.000
Now. not so fast and not so precise.

123
00:09:05.067 --> 00:09:07.667
You don't win with perfect defense.

124
00:09:07.733 --> 00:09:08.867
Lure the attacker.

125
00:09:08.933 --> 00:09:11.000
What?
- Hesitation.

126
00:09:11.067 --> 00:09:13.467
Expose your abdomen to
the enemy's weapon.

127
00:09:13.533 --> 00:09:15.267
His attack is your chance.

128
00:09:47.133 --> 00:09:48.600
Did you teach my dad how to do that?

129
00:09:48.667 --> 00:09:51.267
No. no. He taught me.

130
00:09:51.333 --> 00:09:55.467
You used to say. “You won’t always be
the strongest or the fastest.

131
00:09:55.533 --> 00:09:57.600
"So be the smartest."

132
00:09:58.667 --> 00:10:00.133
And what would he do?

133
00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.600
What if you locked all the suitors in your house?

134
00:10:02.667 --> 00:10:06.133
No. I don't even dare to
imagine what he would do to them.

135
00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:07.733
There are so many of them.

136
00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.000
Take out two. and the rest will back down.

137
00:10:10.067 --> 00:10:12.000
Rude people and cowards.

138
00:10:12.067 --> 00:10:13.733
Many people could have gone to Troy.

139
00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.400
but when Agamemnon arrived.

140
00:10:19.267 --> 00:10:20.600
Their fathers hid them.

141
00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:28.067
And he knew that Agamemnon's war
would take many years and claim many lives.

142
00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.467
And he mustered his army by drawing lots.

143
00:10:38.267 --> 00:10:39.533
Name?
- Sinon.

144
00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:41.267
Pull. No.

145
00:10:41.333 --> 00:10:42.533
Name?
- Antinous.

146
00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:43.600
Weigh.

147
00:10:45.333 --> 00:10:46.333
Yes.

148
00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:47.400
By drawing lots?

149
00:10:50.067 --> 00:10:52.333
Antinoüs told me that he had proposed to her.

150
00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:53.867
I think they're up to something.

151
00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.400
Your son wants to go in place of Sinon.

152
00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:06.800
I won't let him go.

153
00:11:06.867 --> 00:11:08.800
He needs to stay and
take care of his family.

154
00:11:11.667 --> 00:11:13.133
She can take care of your son. too.

155
00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:14.467
Of course.

156
00:11:18.267 --> 00:11:19.533
Stay with me.

157
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.200
Did he want Antinous to take care of me?

158
00:11:22.267 --> 00:11:23.667
No. no. no.

159
00:11:23.733 --> 00:11:26.467
But Odysseus was sensitive
to his father's grief.

160
00:11:26.533 --> 00:11:28.200
He felt sorry for the fathers.

161
00:11:28.267 --> 00:11:31.400
You and your mother meant
more than the promise of glory.

162
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.133
You knew it would take years.

163
00:11:35.733 --> 00:11:37.467
Did you tell my mom?

164
00:11:37.533 --> 00:11:38.933
Ask her.

165
00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.133
Eumeus. will he ever come back?

166
00:11:43.533 --> 00:11:45.067
Argos believes so.

167
00:11:45.133 --> 00:11:47.000
I've never seen a 20-year-old dog.

168
00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:49.800
And he has already outlived his owner.

169
00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.533
I didn't mean to interrupt you.

170
00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:13.000
I'm in no hurry to go anywhere.

171
00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:17.600
According to the elders. when
I finish your grandfather's shroud.

172
00:12:17.667 --> 00:12:19.133
I'll have to get married.

173
00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:20.200
Why?

174
00:12:25.733 --> 00:12:27.800
Ithaca had been without a king for a very long time.

175
00:12:28.467 --> 00:12:30.000
They want protection.

176
00:12:30.067 --> 00:12:31.133
Against what?

177
00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.067
The Maritime Nations.

178
00:12:33.133 --> 00:12:35.400
These are just stories told by travelers.

179
00:12:35.467 --> 00:12:37.267
That's how we learn everything.

180
00:12:37.333 --> 00:12:39.733
That's how we learned about the fall of Troy.

181
00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.267
And now everyone is talking about
the attacks by the seafaring peoples.

182
00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:46.400
And who are the maritime peoples?

183
00:12:46.467 --> 00:12:49.267
No one knows. but the elders
want us to be prepared.

184
00:12:49.333 --> 00:12:50.467
Shall we get ready?

185
00:12:50.533 --> 00:12:53.200
We are part of the largest
known civilization.

186
00:12:55.067 --> 00:12:56.733
Look at that huge house.

187
00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.800
Stones as old as knowledge itself.

188
00:12:58.867 --> 00:13:02.600
Left at the mercy of greedy
guests who are not welcome.

189
00:13:02.667 --> 00:13:05.267
Its construction is nothing

190
00:13:05.333 --> 00:13:07.133
if its meaning is not taken into account.

191
00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:08.867
The Law of Zeus.

192
00:13:09.667 --> 00:13:12.000
Treat others the way
you want to be treated.

193
00:13:12.067 --> 00:13:15.267
They take advantage of the situation and ruin everything.

194
00:13:15.333 --> 00:13:17.800
And how can you even think
about marrying one of them?

195
00:13:17.867 --> 00:13:19.933
Because a long time ago

196
00:13:21.333 --> 00:13:23.467
Your father asked me to do this.

197
00:13:23.533 --> 00:13:25.400
Agamemnon is our king.

198
00:13:25.467 --> 00:13:27.667
And the Trojans abducted his daughter-in-law.

199
00:13:27.733 --> 00:13:29.800
Or she left with them on her own.

200
00:13:29.867 --> 00:13:33.333
Anyway. They say that
the walls of Troy are impregnable.

201
00:13:34.467 --> 00:13:38.800
And we'll sit outside and wait for them.
provoking them to fight.

202
00:13:40.933 --> 00:13:44.067
And so the years go by. And Telemachus grows up.

203
00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:46.867
And you're learning to live without me.

204
00:13:46.933 --> 00:13:49.333
Menelaus may give up on Elena.

205
00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.867
For Agamemnon. this is an excuse.

206
00:13:55.933 --> 00:13:58.933
Troy must stop
controlling the trade routes.

207
00:14:01.067 --> 00:14:04.200
He won't let this chance slip away. Never.

208
00:14:11.133 --> 00:14:13.133
What if you refuse?

209
00:14:19.067 --> 00:14:20.600
He's going to take our son.

210
00:14:22.333 --> 00:14:23.400
He wouldn't do that.

211
00:14:24.133 --> 00:14:27.600
He sacrifices his own
daughter for a favorable wind.

212
00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.267
Why do the gods want his daughter?

213
00:14:32.133 --> 00:14:37.800
The power of self-sacrifice lies
in how much it costs.

214
00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.000
It's monstrous.

215
00:14:45.667 --> 00:14:47.000
And he shows determination.

216
00:14:50.133 --> 00:14:51.667
I won't be back anytime soon.

217
00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:53.933
If I ever come back at all.

218
00:14:58.600 --> 00:14:59.933
What if we run away?

219
00:15:01.667 --> 00:15:03.467
On your fastest ship.

220
00:15:03.533 --> 00:15:05.200
With your best crew.

221
00:15:05.267 --> 00:15:07.400
And we set off toward the horizon.

222
00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:13.467
Holding hands on
the deck. rowing toward the sunset.

223
00:15:13.533 --> 00:15:15.400
chasing the setting sun.

224
00:15:19.067 --> 00:15:20.800
There's more to it than meets the eye.

225
00:15:21.733 --> 00:15:27.133
Agamemnon's vengeance
will come down upon all of Ithaca.

226
00:15:33.333 --> 00:15:34.533
Promise me that.

227
00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:38.867
If I don't return

228
00:15:38.933 --> 00:15:40.933
and when Telemachus came of age.

229
00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:44.467
Promise me.

230
00:15:44.533 --> 00:15:46.267
that you'll get married again.

231
00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:50.000
Enough with the sacrifices.

232
00:15:50.067 --> 00:15:51.667
Love is not a sacrifice.

233
00:15:51.733 --> 00:15:53.000
Of course it is.

234
00:15:54.333 --> 00:15:55.667
And I don't want you to do that.

235
00:15:55.733 --> 00:15:57.133
It's not up to you.

236
00:15:59.467 --> 00:16:00.733
Promise me that.

237
00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.933
Promise me you'll come back.

238
00:16:14.533 --> 00:16:15.733
What if I can't?

239
00:16:21.733 --> 00:16:24.533
Then the memory of you
won't make decisions for me.

240
00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.333
Take my brooch.

241
00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:33.067
Always carry it with you.

242
00:16:33.133 --> 00:16:35.133
Athens will bring you back home.

243
00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:36.733
I don't think it would suit me.

244
00:16:39.267 --> 00:16:41.867
When passengers say they've seen them…

245
00:16:41.933 --> 00:16:43.933
I'll ask them what you were wearing.

246
00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:45.667
That way I'll know if they're lying.

247
00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:52.067
If the sea peoples exist.

248
00:16:52.133 --> 00:16:55.933
At the moment. Ithaca has no
king who could raise an army.

249
00:16:56.533 --> 00:16:58.000
I… I could take the throne.

250
00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.200
Come.

251
00:17:13.133 --> 00:17:14.400
Place the bowstring on the bow.

252
00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:26.333
That's not fair.

253
00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:28.333
He said that only he could do it.

254
00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.267
He liked it when others tried.

255
00:17:32.733 --> 00:17:34.400
You were putting all the axes in order.

256
00:17:35.933 --> 00:17:37.067
Yes.

257
00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:08.200
No one has ever succeeded.

258
00:18:09.667 --> 00:18:11.133
Mom. they're ripping us off.

259
00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:12.733
to choose

260
00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:15.467
one of them. before he ascended the throne.

261
00:18:15.533 --> 00:18:18.133
My companion told me that Menelaus
had returned years ago.

262
00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:19.400
Everyone came back.

263
00:18:19.467 --> 00:18:21.067
Those who survived.

264
00:18:22.533 --> 00:18:23.533
He may have died.

265
00:18:24.467 --> 00:18:26.733
If that's the case. this is my palace.

266
00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:28.933
We need to understand.

267
00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:31.467
I need to go see Menelaus and find out.

268
00:18:31.533 --> 00:18:33.667
Telemachus.

269
00:18:33.733 --> 00:18:35.267
The suitors want to harm you.

270
00:18:35.333 --> 00:18:37.667
Do you think I don't know?

271
00:18:37.733 --> 00:18:40.267
Every evening. they wait for me to give them a reason.

272
00:18:40.333 --> 00:18:43.467
And outside the home. out at sea. on the roads?

273
00:18:44.133 --> 00:18:46.467
They won't be short of
reasons. You won't be coming back.

274
00:18:48.667 --> 00:18:50.933
You don't want me to lay claim to the throne.

275
00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:56.733
You want to keep me in my place
so you can remain the queen of Ithaca.

276
00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:59.667
I'm tired of being queen
of Ithaca without my king!

277
00:18:59.733 --> 00:19:02.467
Without your king?

278
00:19:02.533 --> 00:19:03.600
Or without "some" king?

279
00:19:05.133 --> 00:19:08.133
Do you think you can
take your father's place?

280
00:19:09.933 --> 00:19:11.333
You're just a kid.

281
00:19:21.733 --> 00:19:23.467
What a commotion. Melantius.

282
00:19:23.533 --> 00:19:25.267
Don't step in with your spurs.

283
00:19:25.333 --> 00:19:27.400
You're a cowherd. not a horse herder.

284
00:19:27.467 --> 00:19:31.333
And you're a pig. Who do
you think you are. talking to me like that?

285
00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:35.200
The servant whom your master
has put in charge of his shepherds.

286
00:19:35.267 --> 00:19:38.933
Our master
left a long time ago. you blind fool.

287
00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.667
And you're just as welcome as that dog.

288
00:19:42.867 --> 00:19:43.867
Argos!

289
00:19:47.333 --> 00:19:48.667
Argos!

290
00:19:49.867 --> 00:19:51.267
Argos.

291
00:19:51.333 --> 00:19:52.400
Argos.

292
00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:55.533
Master. he hasn't done anything.

293
00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:57.067
Is that you who stinks?

294
00:19:58.267 --> 00:19:59.733
Leave the dog alone.

295
00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:01.600
Which one?
- Both of us.

296
00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:04.933
What did you say. slave?
- He got carried away.

297
00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:06.867
It was my father's hunting dog.

298
00:20:06.933 --> 00:20:08.467
His hunting dog?

299
00:20:12.333 --> 00:20:13.467
Well. look at that.

300
00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.000
Bring it out. We want to eat.

301
00:20:16.067 --> 00:20:18.200
No! Can't you see that he's in pain?

302
00:20:18.267 --> 00:20:21.267
All right. I'll put him out of his misery.

303
00:20:21.333 --> 00:20:22.533
Leave him alone!

304
00:20:40.133 --> 00:20:41.200
Did you see that?

305
00:20:42.467 --> 00:20:43.533
Have you seen it yet?

306
00:20:44.867 --> 00:20:46.733
He attacked me!

307
00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:47.867
I have the right!

308
00:20:48.467 --> 00:20:49.533
Please!

309
00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.133
Please don't hurt my son.

310
00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:58.533
Okay. Okay. Polib.

311
00:20:59.267 --> 00:21:01.467
He's lost his mind. Have mercy.

312
00:21:02.133 --> 00:21:05.200
Look. Have some more of his wine.

313
00:21:05.267 --> 00:21:06.667
I'll take care of it.

314
00:21:07.333 --> 00:21:09.133
That was his hunting dog.

315
00:21:10.333 --> 00:21:12.267
She lives only to see Odysseus return.

316
00:21:12.333 --> 00:21:13.333
Okay.

317
00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:14.867
He goes to the outhouse.

318
00:21:14.933 --> 00:21:16.933
so that he could be the first to greet his master.

319
00:21:27.267 --> 00:21:28.933
The dog stays outside.

320
00:21:30.867 --> 00:21:33.533
Think carefully before
taking a risk for an animal.

321
00:21:45.333 --> 00:21:46.333
Euriclea.

322
00:21:48.267 --> 00:21:50.133
Deliver provisions to the ships.

323
00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:51.267
No one should know.

324
00:21:51.333 --> 00:21:52.667
Surround yourself with trustworthy people.

325
00:21:52.733 --> 00:21:54.400
You're going to break your mother's heart.

326
00:21:54.467 --> 00:21:56.400
If I stay. they'll make me fight.

327
00:21:56.467 --> 00:21:58.000
No one will support me.

328
00:21:58.067 --> 00:21:59.800
I need to find out about my father.

329
00:21:59.867 --> 00:22:03.400
So it will help me if
they realize that Odysseus is dead.

330
00:22:03.467 --> 00:22:05.000
The grooms won't let that happen.

331
00:22:05.067 --> 00:22:06.800
You won't come back alive.

332
00:22:06.867 --> 00:22:09.667
Tell Mentor to wait for me
on the ships at dawn.

333
00:22:10.333 --> 00:22:13.067
What do you want me to tell your mom?

334
00:22:13.933 --> 00:22:15.800
To last as long as possible.

335
00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:21.267
The ship is ready. The tide is favorable.

336
00:22:22.133 --> 00:22:24.067
Have I ever told you that you have wise eyes?

337
00:22:24.133 --> 00:22:26.067
The Eyes of Athens?

338
00:22:26.133 --> 00:22:27.267
A few times.

339
00:22:27.333 --> 00:22:29.333
He didn't ask where we were going.

340
00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:31.000
Where are we going?

341
00:22:31.067 --> 00:22:32.133
Let's find my dad.

342
00:22:32.733 --> 00:22:35.000
He's alive. I can tell by the wind and the waves.

343
00:22:35.067 --> 00:22:36.533
It's out there somewhere.

344
00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:38.733
wandering. wounded. lost—I don't know.

345
00:22:39.933 --> 00:22:41.467
But he's alive.

346
00:23:12.933 --> 00:23:14.200
More dreams?

347
00:23:15.533 --> 00:23:16.733
Generous people.

348
00:23:17.267 --> 00:23:18.333
Closed.

349
00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:22.867
In the darkness.

350
00:23:25.667 --> 00:23:27.667
The reflection of a fire on a rock. I don't know.

351
00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:32.267
Don't you want to remember?

352
00:23:32.333 --> 00:23:35.467
Why don't the gods speak
in a way that we can understand them?

353
00:23:38.067 --> 00:23:39.867
Who doesn't understand the thunder?

354
00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:42.200
Or the fire?

355
00:23:42.267 --> 00:23:45.133
A child's smile. a bountiful harvest?

356
00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:47.267
But they're hiding from us.

357
00:23:48.867 --> 00:23:49.867
Look.

358
00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:52.067
Who runs the cloud?

359
00:23:53.067 --> 00:23:54.733
Is he calling the birds?

360
00:23:56.067 --> 00:23:57.400
Who controls the water?

361
00:23:59.467 --> 00:24:00.933
Who were you talking to?

362
00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:05.267
Athena came to see me again.

363
00:24:05.933 --> 00:24:07.867
He tells me to remember.

364
00:24:09.533 --> 00:24:10.667
I'd better go.

365
00:24:10.733 --> 00:24:11.867
Why would you leave?

366
00:24:11.933 --> 00:24:13.933
We have everything we need.

367
00:24:14.867 --> 00:24:16.733
How long have I been here. Calypso?

368
00:24:21.333 --> 00:24:22.533
I have a home.

369
00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:26.600
There's someone there.

370
00:24:26.667 --> 00:24:28.333
There's someone there.

371
00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:30.000
How do you know?

372
00:24:30.067 --> 00:24:31.333
I don't know.

373
00:24:33.867 --> 00:24:34.867
I'm sorry.

374
00:24:51.067 --> 00:24:53.067
What we have may last forever.

375
00:24:53.133 --> 00:24:54.600
Eternity is for the gods.

376
00:24:54.667 --> 00:24:56.000
Are you a goddess?

377
00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:02.533
You want to remember. but what
if that puts an end to your happiness?

378
00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.067
So it wasn't real.

379
00:25:11.733 --> 00:25:12.600
No.

380
00:25:12.667 --> 00:25:14.467
Not anymore.
- But I like it.

381
00:25:15.067 --> 00:25:17.133
Stop eating from the lotus.

382
00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:19.133
Tell me what you remember.

383
00:25:20.733 --> 00:25:21.733
After Troy.

384
00:25:23.333 --> 00:25:24.333
Troy.

385
00:25:31.067 --> 00:25:32.533
We won the war.

386
00:25:33.133 --> 00:25:34.467
And then what?

387
00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:41.667
Ten years on this lousy beach.

388
00:26:46.667 --> 00:26:47.667
Let's go home.

389
00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:51.600
Remember those we left behind.

390
00:26:56.267 --> 00:26:57.667
Will the provisions be enough?

391
00:26:58.533 --> 00:27:00.067
We've brought quite a bit from Troy.

392
00:27:01.133 --> 00:27:02.600
One more stop won't kill us.

393
00:27:04.267 --> 00:27:06.400
And… pull!

394
00:27:07.333 --> 00:27:09.400
And… pull!

395
00:27:10.467 --> 00:27:12.733
And… pull!

396
00:27:14.933 --> 00:27:16.200
Put the oars away.

397
00:27:16.267 --> 00:27:17.267
Break!

398
00:27:17.333 --> 00:27:18.467
Break!

399
00:27:18.533 --> 00:27:19.667
Put away the oars!

400
00:27:19.733 --> 00:27:21.333
Put away the oars!

401
00:27:26.267 --> 00:27:28.667
Hoist the sail so we can
follow the southerly wind.

402
00:27:28.733 --> 00:27:29.733
That's so true.

403
00:27:30.667 --> 00:27:33.067
Agamemnon sailed westward along a safe route.

404
00:27:33.133 --> 00:27:34.667
He brings more people along with him.

405
00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:37.067
We'll find a place to stay.

406
00:27:37.133 --> 00:27:38.467
Is that so? That's risky.

407
00:27:38.533 --> 00:27:40.133
I had followed Agamemnon long enough.

408
00:27:41.133 --> 00:27:43.400
With a little luck and a favorable wind

409
00:27:43.467 --> 00:27:45.200
We'll see the world. after all

410
00:27:45.267 --> 00:27:47.200
We'll get there before Agamemnon.

411
00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:51.600
Quiet!

412
00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:58.400
Keep to the right.

413
00:29:08.333 --> 00:29:09.400
Odysseus!

414
00:29:09.467 --> 00:29:10.733
Earth!

415
00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:15.133
Do you see?

416
00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:16.533
That damn lucky guy.

417
00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:19.267
We're running out of supplies.

418
00:29:19.333 --> 00:29:20.800
Prepare an advance party.

419
00:29:20.867 --> 00:29:23.333
Food and water. For three weeks.

420
00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:24.600
What if they don't give it to us?

421
00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:28.533
How much do you want to go back?

422
00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:31.000
Get your weapons ready!

423
00:29:31.067 --> 00:29:32.333
Sifting.

424
00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:33.400
asking for a loan.

425
00:29:34.267 --> 00:29:35.333
grabbing.

426
00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:42.333
Ask why they ran away and didn't come out to greet us.

427
00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:50.867
They think of us as a seafaring people.

428
00:29:50.933 --> 00:29:52.000
No.

429
00:29:52.067 --> 00:29:54.600
We are Greeks. From Ithaca.

430
00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:57.267
Did you know we won at Troy?

431
00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:00.200
We're heading west with the south wind.

432
00:30:07.067 --> 00:30:08.867
He says that if we continue down this path.

433
00:30:08.933 --> 00:30:10.567
We won't like what we find.

434
00:30:12.067 --> 00:30:14.667
After years of war

435
00:30:14.733 --> 00:30:18.733
Nothing could come
between my people and their home.

436
00:30:19.533 --> 00:30:20.867
Even me.

437
00:30:22.133 --> 00:30:24.000
And then something happened.

438
00:30:39.867 --> 00:30:41.533
Chasing a fugitive.

439
00:30:51.067 --> 00:30:52.067
That damn lucky guy.

440
00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:06.400
No. no. no.

441
00:31:06.467 --> 00:31:08.667
Sheep don't walk on their own.

442
00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:11.000
Let's follow her.

443
00:32:31.667 --> 00:32:33.133
Someone lives here.

444
00:32:34.333 --> 00:32:35.533
Yes. He makes cheese.

445
00:32:54.333 --> 00:32:55.600
It's pretty good.

446
00:32:58.333 --> 00:32:59.733
There are many.

447
00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:01.067
We have to leave.

448
00:33:01.133 --> 00:33:03.200
We have nothing to trade.

449
00:33:03.800 --> 00:33:05.267
The Law of Zeus.

450
00:33:05.333 --> 00:33:07.600
I don't know if the person who lives here knows that.

451
00:33:07.667 --> 00:33:08.867
Or Zeus.

452
00:33:08.933 --> 00:33:11.133
Everyone knows about the Law of Zeus.

453
00:33:11.200 --> 00:33:12.600
Light the fire!

454
00:33:12.667 --> 00:33:14.467
We'll greet the host.

455
00:34:33.467 --> 00:34:34.800
Stay away from the fire.

456
00:34:39.467 --> 00:34:41.133
Spread out!

457
00:36:13.933 --> 00:36:15.400
I think he's asleep.

458
00:37:00.267 --> 00:37:01.267
Wait.

459
00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:06.067
The Rock.

460
00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:14.800
We can't move it.

461
00:37:14.867 --> 00:37:16.133
We're trapped.

462
00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:25.733
Pass it on.

463
00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:28.333
In the morning. when he takes the herd out.

464
00:37:29.267 --> 00:37:30.867
We ran toward the exit.

465
00:37:30.933 --> 00:37:32.000
Take a break.

466
00:37:56.800 --> 00:37:57.800
Come on. come on!

467
00:38:09.333 --> 00:38:10.333
Run!

468
00:38:18.467 --> 00:38:21.333
Can we wedge something into the rock?

469
00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:23.267
To leave a gap?

470
00:38:23.333 --> 00:38:25.400
Maybe.

471
00:38:25.467 --> 00:38:26.733
But what?

472
00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:27.800
That guy.

473
00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:32.867
He'll notice.

474
00:38:34.467 --> 00:38:37.933
Grab it by the end. Put it in the fire.

475
00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:39.133
How many ropes are there?

476
00:38:40.067 --> 00:38:41.200
How many sheep?

477
00:38:41.267 --> 00:38:42.467
Two dozen.

478
00:38:45.067 --> 00:38:46.933
There are a lot of us.

479
00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:49.400
When he comes back. the two of us have to leave.

480
00:38:49.467 --> 00:38:51.000
Or be killed.

481
00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:59.267
Run in a crouched position through
the herd. and then scatter.

482
00:38:59.333 --> 00:39:00.733
Just to catch one.

483
00:39:01.667 --> 00:39:02.067
Yes.

484
00:39:02.133 --> 00:39:03.467
The four of us have to run.

485
00:39:03.533 --> 00:39:04.600
Two by two.

486
00:39:04.667 --> 00:39:06.267
Why take any more risks?

487
00:39:06.333 --> 00:39:07.733
He ate two last night.

488
00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:09.533
He'll eat two more before he goes to sleep.

489
00:39:34.467 --> 00:39:35.533
Run. run!

490
00:41:13.933 --> 00:41:15.067
And what about his body?

491
00:41:15.133 --> 00:41:16.533
What about him?

492
00:41:16.600 --> 00:41:19.400
Are we just going to leave it there to rot?

493
00:41:19.467 --> 00:41:20.933
So that thing can eat it?

494
00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:25.533
We honor our dead. Odysseus.

495
00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:28.733
He died helping us escape.
We honor him by running.

496
00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:36.000
Father Poseidon. they've blinded me.

497
00:41:36.067 --> 00:41:39.667
Revenge. Dad. Revenge for my pain.

498
00:41:39.733 --> 00:41:42.867
Poseidon. avenge my pain.

499
00:41:42.933 --> 00:41:45.400
He can speak.

500
00:41:45.467 --> 00:41:47.000
Why didn't he speak up sooner?

501
00:41:48.467 --> 00:41:50.133
Do you talk to ants?

502
00:41:51.533 --> 00:41:53.133
Should we work things out with him?

503
00:41:55.600 --> 00:41:57.133
I think it's already too late.

504
00:43:08.533 --> 00:43:09.533
What are you doing?

505
00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:11.800
No!

506
00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:31.533
Come on. Run. run!

507
00:43:31.600 --> 00:43:32.600
Let's go!

508
00:44:01.200 --> 00:44:02.200
Hop on!

509
00:44:04.267 --> 00:44:05.267
Let's go!

510
00:44:26.667 --> 00:44:28.200
Come on. come on!
- Come on!

511
00:44:29.933 --> 00:44:32.133
Let's go!

512
00:44:52.733 --> 00:44:54.533
Why is he shooting?

513
00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:56.733
That beast killed good soldiers.

514
00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:00.067
Yes. and that beast was also the son of Poseidon.

515
00:45:00.133 --> 00:45:01.133
What?

516
00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:03.800
I heard him call out when
we gouged out his eye.

517
00:45:03.867 --> 00:45:05.467
That thing was a monster.

518
00:45:05.533 --> 00:45:07.400
I heard the same thing. too.

519
00:45:07.467 --> 00:45:09.467
Is the sea against us now. too?

520
00:45:09.533 --> 00:45:11.467
And the wind.

521
00:45:11.533 --> 00:45:14.200
How will we get home
with Poseidon against us?

522
00:45:14.267 --> 00:45:16.733
We cannot live solely
on omens and sacrifices.

523
00:45:18.867 --> 00:45:20.933
Do you know how many times I heard you praying?

524
00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:23.200
to Athens. since we left Troy?

525
00:45:23.267 --> 00:45:24.733
Couldn't you have shown some mercy?

526
00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:27.200
Take it.

527
00:45:29.800 --> 00:45:31.067
Put it on.

528
00:45:31.133 --> 00:45:33.267
So that you may remember those we did not bury.

529
00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:36.800
The gods help
those who help themselves.

530
00:45:40.533 --> 00:45:44.267
Ever since then. every stormy sea and headwind

531
00:45:44.333 --> 00:45:46.000
You told them that we were cursed.

532
00:45:48.467 --> 00:45:50.067
But where is my crew?

533
00:45:51.667 --> 00:45:54.467
When will you let me see them?

534
00:45:54.533 --> 00:45:55.867
If I were talking to them.

535
00:45:55.933 --> 00:45:57.067
Maybe I would have remembered.

536
00:45:57.133 --> 00:45:58.267
Did I have a husband?

537
00:45:58.333 --> 00:45:59.800
Children? Maybe a son.

538
00:45:59.867 --> 00:46:01.600
How long have I been here?

539
00:46:03.200 --> 00:46:04.400
Quite a while.

540
00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:06.600
If I had a son.

541
00:46:08.533 --> 00:46:10.000
How old would he be?

542
00:46:12.133 --> 00:46:13.400
I don't know how to talk to a king.

543
00:46:14.200 --> 00:46:15.467
You'll do just fine.

544
00:46:15.533 --> 00:46:17.733
Just don't say a word.

545
00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:21.200
But if anyone asks. we're merchants from Crete.

546
00:46:21.267 --> 00:46:22.933
We'll approach the king.

547
00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:34.533
Welcome.

548
00:46:34.600 --> 00:46:36.667
Thank you. That's all…

549
00:46:36.733 --> 00:46:38.000
From here.

550
00:46:43.133 --> 00:46:44.400
Where are they taking us?

551
00:46:44.467 --> 00:46:47.000
The king is determined to meet with the foreigners.

552
00:47:08.467 --> 00:47:10.133
Sit down.

553
00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:14.000
Not you.

554
00:47:15.333 --> 00:47:16.333
You.

555
00:47:19.267 --> 00:47:20.733
Enjoy the hospitality.

556
00:47:20.800 --> 00:47:22.600
As Zeus commands.

557
00:47:22.667 --> 00:47:25.933
And then. maybe. you'll tell us who you are.

558
00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:27.600
It's a nice day to come.

559
00:47:27.667 --> 00:47:29.667
It's a celebration. My daughter is getting married.

560
00:47:29.733 --> 00:47:33.133
Tomorrow he's leaving to start a new life.

561
00:47:33.200 --> 00:47:35.267
You don't look like a beggar.

562
00:47:35.333 --> 00:47:38.467
I guess you know who I am.

563
00:47:38.533 --> 00:47:41.733
I am Menelaus. son of Atreus.

564
00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:43.467
Agamemnon's brother.

565
00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:46.133
Elena's husband.

566
00:47:46.667 --> 00:47:47.733
You've heard of Elena.

567
00:47:50.600 --> 00:47:54.400
The person for whom
a thousand ships set sail.

568
00:47:56.133 --> 00:47:58.067
Today. maybe just five hundred.

569
00:48:02.667 --> 00:48:05.800
So many died on
the plains of Troy because of him.

570
00:48:05.867 --> 00:48:08.000
Because of your brother's ambition.

571
00:48:08.867 --> 00:48:10.733
This one looks like someone.

572
00:48:11.933 --> 00:48:12.933
Ajax…

573
00:48:13.933 --> 00:48:15.000
Patroclus…

574
00:48:15.067 --> 00:48:17.000
Achilles…

575
00:48:17.067 --> 00:48:20.467
I don't miss them as much
as I miss one particular warrior.

576
00:48:21.133 --> 00:48:23.067
The smartest of us all.

577
00:48:25.333 --> 00:48:27.467
We've been rotting away on that shore for ten years.

578
00:48:40.933 --> 00:48:42.400
In a stalemate.

579
00:48:42.467 --> 00:48:44.533
Arguing while one

580
00:48:47.800 --> 00:48:48.867
He couldn't figure it out.

581
00:48:50.933 --> 00:48:52.067
Odysseus.

582
00:48:53.267 --> 00:48:54.500
Have you heard the story about the horse?

583
00:48:58.133 --> 00:48:59.467
Is that someone from over there?

584
00:49:04.267 --> 00:49:05.600
We were there for days.

585
00:49:06.933 --> 00:49:09.600
Crammed in before the ship set sail.

586
00:49:10.933 --> 00:49:13.400
He knew. No one
was supposed to know we were inside.

587
00:49:13.467 --> 00:49:16.133
Nor Sinon. the soldier we left behind.

588
00:49:21.800 --> 00:49:24.600
During the first high tides.
some of us drowned.

589
00:49:30.333 --> 00:49:33.267
We were there. silent. surrounded by urine and feces.

590
00:50:01.400 --> 00:50:03.133
A gift.

591
00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:04.400
About Athens.

592
00:50:06.933 --> 00:50:08.133
We have gods in common.

593
00:50:09.067 --> 00:50:10.200
Do you appreciate the gift?

594
00:51:31.933 --> 00:51:34.133
We have to burn it.

595
00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:36.400
Burn him! Burn him!

596
00:51:58.533 --> 00:51:59.867
This is a gift for Athens!

597
00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:11.200
A Gift for Athens!

598
00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:48.000
The ports!

599
00:54:49.467 --> 00:54:53.733
I knew we had to
wait for a moonless night in Troy.

600
00:55:00.400 --> 00:55:01.733
Come on. come on!

601
00:55:07.000 --> 00:55:09.133
They're attacking the city!
- To the gates!

602
00:55:09.733 --> 00:55:11.000
To the gates!

603
00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:31.000
Run. run!

604
00:55:32.267 --> 00:55:34.067
The city is under attack!

605
00:56:45.600 --> 00:56:46.667
Let's go!

606
00:56:46.733 --> 00:56:47.733
Come on. come on!

607
00:57:20.267 --> 00:57:22.533
And you already know the rest.

608
00:57:22.600 --> 00:57:25.800
Does my story move you?
You should hear the bards.

609
00:57:25.867 --> 00:57:27.067
I've already heard them.

610
00:57:30.400 --> 00:57:32.133
But they weren't with you.

611
00:57:32.200 --> 00:57:33.867
With your father.

612
00:57:33.933 --> 00:57:34.933
Odysseus.

613
00:57:38.000 --> 00:57:39.267
You look so much like him.

614
00:57:40.667 --> 00:57:41.867
Where did you say goodbye?

615
00:57:41.933 --> 00:57:43.067
In Troy.

616
00:57:44.333 --> 00:57:45.800
The gods granted us a just victory.

617
00:57:45.867 --> 00:57:47.067
Fair…

618
00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:51.867
They gave us a well-deserved victory.
without blessing our journeys.

619
00:57:53.067 --> 00:57:55.133
It took me years to return home.

620
00:57:55.200 --> 00:57:56.267
But he didn't come back.

621
00:57:56.333 --> 00:57:58.000
Others returned and regretted it.

622
00:57:59.133 --> 00:58:00.267
Tell him.

623
00:58:00.333 --> 00:58:02.400
Tell him about your brother.
- No.

624
00:58:02.467 --> 00:58:05.800
For what the gods
had decreed for him after the just battle.

625
00:58:05.867 --> 00:58:07.667
No.
- For welcoming him as a hero.

626
00:58:07.733 --> 00:58:09.800
No.
- How Agamemnon Returned Home

627
00:58:09.867 --> 00:58:11.200
years ahead of us.
- No.

628
00:58:11.267 --> 00:58:13.133
How did his queen receive him.

629
00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:15.133
my twin sister. Clytemnestra.

630
00:58:15.200 --> 00:58:16.733
No. No.
- Tell him that she…

631
00:58:16.800 --> 00:58:19.667
No! He doesn't want to know about my brother.

632
00:58:22.333 --> 00:58:23.667
And as for his father.

633
00:58:24.800 --> 00:58:27.200
I don't know anything about Odysseus since he left Troy.

634
00:58:27.267 --> 00:58:29.600
Nothing?

635
00:58:29.667 --> 00:58:32.067
Not even any of your people?

636
00:58:32.133 --> 00:58:34.800
Nor those of Agamemnon.
the merchants. the travelers?

637
00:58:34.867 --> 00:58:38.467
Do you want rumors? Is that what you want? Gossip?

638
00:58:38.533 --> 00:58:40.000
You're in the right place.

639
00:58:40.067 --> 00:58:43.333
Does anyone want to tell
a story about Odysseus?

640
00:58:43.400 --> 00:58:46.200
Some say he's rich.
Others say he's poor.

641
00:58:47.333 --> 00:58:49.800
Some say he is dead.
Others say he is in prison.

642
00:58:49.867 --> 00:58:51.400
What do you think?
- Locked up?

643
00:58:54.733 --> 00:58:56.867
Which prison?

644
00:58:56.933 --> 00:58:58.333
Would you arrest a man like that?

645
00:58:59.733 --> 00:59:01.000
Tell me what you remember.

646
00:59:02.000 --> 00:59:03.133
The rest will fall into place.

647
00:59:04.333 --> 00:59:08.067
The men were convinced
that he had offended Poseidon.

648
00:59:28.333 --> 00:59:29.533
All set!

649
00:59:29.600 --> 00:59:30.867
All set!

650
00:59:32.000 --> 00:59:33.800
Pull!

651
00:59:33.867 --> 00:59:36.733
And… pull!

652
00:59:37.867 --> 00:59:40.267
And… pull!

653
00:59:51.733 --> 00:59:53.733
Pull!

654
00:59:53.800 --> 00:59:55.933
We faced one storm after another.

655
00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:58.000
Off Course

656
00:59:58.067 --> 01:00:00.133
until we really got lost.

657
01:00:03.800 --> 01:00:06.400
And… pull!

658
01:00:06.467 --> 01:00:08.667
And… pull!

659
01:00:11.133 --> 01:00:12.133
Pull!

660
01:00:13.600 --> 01:00:16.867
And… pull!

661
01:00:16.933 --> 01:00:19.067
Row!

662
01:00:52.333 --> 01:00:53.600
Hi.

663
01:00:55.467 --> 01:00:56.533
We come in peace.

664
01:00:59.267 --> 01:01:00.867
All we need is food.

665
01:01:06.800 --> 01:01:07.933
We need food.

666
01:01:09.933 --> 01:01:12.000
Do you understand me?
- It's just a child.

667
01:01:14.600 --> 01:01:17.267
If this is a child. what must the parents be like?

668
01:01:19.600 --> 01:01:21.267
Hey. It's nothing.

669
01:01:21.933 --> 01:01:22.933
No. no. no.

670
01:01:43.067 --> 01:01:45.133
Run! Run!

671
01:01:45.200 --> 01:01:47.467
To the ship!

672
01:02:01.933 --> 01:02:03.400
Come on. come on. Come on.

673
01:02:12.133 --> 01:02:14.200
Back! Back! Come on.

674
01:03:23.933 --> 01:03:25.533
Run! Hurry up!

675
01:03:34.933 --> 01:03:37.067
Give me the bow! My bow!

676
01:03:41.267 --> 01:03:42.267
Let's go!

677
01:03:52.267 --> 01:03:54.133
Come on! Come on!

678
01:03:54.200 --> 01:03:55.467
Row!

679
01:03:55.533 --> 01:03:56.533
Wait!

680
01:03:57.600 --> 01:03:59.133
And… pull!

681
01:03:59.200 --> 01:04:00.267
Wait!

682
01:04:00.333 --> 01:04:02.267
And… pull!

683
01:04:02.867 --> 01:04:05.067
And… pull!

684
01:04:05.867 --> 01:04:07.867
And… pull!

685
01:04:07.933 --> 01:04:09.000
Let's go!

686
01:04:09.533 --> 01:04:11.000
Wait!
- Pull!

687
01:04:13.067 --> 01:04:14.067
Wait!

688
01:04:24.400 --> 01:04:25.867
The oars!

689
01:04:31.533 --> 01:04:34.867
All right! Get moving!

690
01:05:28.467 --> 01:05:29.733
To the left.

691
01:05:29.800 --> 01:05:31.333
Follow the shore.

692
01:05:31.933 --> 01:05:33.067
What if they have ships?

693
01:05:33.133 --> 01:05:34.867
We need to get as far away as we can.

694
01:05:39.733 --> 01:05:42.667
We don't have the resources to sail blindly.

695
01:05:42.733 --> 01:05:44.667
We lost our way.

696
01:05:44.733 --> 01:05:47.733
We need to find some food
and regroup.

697
01:05:50.333 --> 01:05:51.333
Port side.

698
01:06:18.233 --> 01:06:19.933
There!

699
01:06:20.000 --> 01:06:20.933
Smoke!

700
01:06:21.000 --> 01:06:21.267
There!

701
01:06:21.333 --> 01:06:24.133
Head over there; there'll be a place to 
drop anchor on the beach

702
01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:32.400
The oars!

703
01:06:36.600 --> 01:06:38.267
Form an assault team.

704
01:06:40.200 --> 01:06:41.200
Tomorrow!

705
01:06:43.933 --> 01:06:45.133
Tomorrow!

706
01:06:55.600 --> 01:06:57.200
Have someone keep watch.

707
01:06:58.333 --> 01:06:59.867
We want you to stay.

708
01:07:02.267 --> 01:07:04.533
Who?
- All of us.

709
01:07:04.600 --> 01:07:05.933
Those of us who are still alive.

710
01:07:16.600 --> 01:07:18.067
Is this a rebellion?

711
01:07:18.133 --> 01:07:19.533
It's up to you.

712
01:07:23.667 --> 01:07:24.800
Give me your bow.

713
01:07:24.867 --> 01:07:26.733
I'll make sure we have dinner.

714
01:07:26.800 --> 01:07:28.800
It doesn't matter what you find.

715
01:09:15.467 --> 01:09:17.467
No. no! Everything's fine!

716
01:09:21.400 --> 01:09:22.533
Don't hurt me!
- Calm down.

717
01:09:22.600 --> 01:09:24.000
You're right!
- No!

718
01:09:24.067 --> 01:09:25.200
We won't hurt you!

719
01:09:25.267 --> 01:09:26.667
If I do what you say.

720
01:09:26.733 --> 01:09:29.600
No one is going to hurt you.

721
01:09:32.933 --> 01:09:34.800
I'm Evriloch. What's your name?

722
01:09:34.867 --> 01:09:36.000
Circe.

723
01:09:36.067 --> 01:09:39.000
Circe. we need food. Right away.

724
01:09:40.067 --> 01:09:41.267
I have some food.

725
01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:44.533
A lot of food.

726
01:10:05.733 --> 01:10:06.867
Let me.

727
01:10:10.000 --> 01:10:11.867
Weapons. armor

728
01:10:13.400 --> 01:10:14.533
Put them in the back.

729
01:10:57.533 --> 01:10:59.333
Do you like my meat?

730
01:10:59.867 --> 01:11:03.267
Why… do you have so many?

731
01:11:03.333 --> 01:11:05.667
You never know when
strangers might show up.

732
01:11:15.667 --> 01:11:16.667
Men.

733
01:11:19.533 --> 01:11:20.733
With their appetites.

734
01:11:22.933 --> 01:11:25.000
"Hard" is never enough.

735
01:12:40.667 --> 01:12:42.467
Eurylochus!

736
01:13:07.800 --> 01:13:09.067
They didn't come back.

737
01:13:09.133 --> 01:13:10.467
No one?
- No.

738
01:13:10.533 --> 01:13:11.667
Give me your knife.

739
01:13:15.133 --> 01:13:16.267
Stay right there!

740
01:13:57.733 --> 01:13:59.200
Why don't you come closer?

741
01:14:07.600 --> 01:14:09.333
Are you coming from the sea?

742
01:14:11.133 --> 01:14:12.467
Are you alone?

743
01:14:17.467 --> 01:14:20.067
My pigs liked you.

744
01:14:20.600 --> 01:14:22.133
Are you hungry?

745
01:14:24.400 --> 01:14:26.067
Leave your weapons by the door.

746
01:14:26.133 --> 01:14:28.533
Enjoy Zeus's hospitality.

747
01:14:29.867 --> 01:14:32.933
Get a pig. You can
slaughter it for dinner.

748
01:14:57.800 --> 01:15:00.533
Whoever you are. you're obviously not a farmer.

749
01:15:00.600 --> 01:15:01.600
Give it to me.

750
01:15:05.267 --> 01:15:06.800
The stew smells good.

751
01:15:10.400 --> 01:15:11.500
That's enough for both of us.

752
01:15:13.200 --> 01:15:14.800
Who says there are two of us?

753
01:15:16.733 --> 01:15:17.867
Who else is here?

754
01:15:17.933 --> 01:15:19.267
Who. right?

755
01:15:32.533 --> 01:15:34.000
You say you're alone.

756
01:15:34.800 --> 01:15:36.467
But no one comes here alone.

757
01:15:37.400 --> 01:15:38.800
Right?

758
01:15:38.867 --> 01:15:41.333
You can only get there by sea.

759
01:15:41.933 --> 01:15:44.400
Where are your companions?

760
01:15:45.133 --> 01:15:46.467
I'm looking for them.

761
01:15:47.400 --> 01:15:48.800
Has anyone been here?

762
01:15:52.200 --> 01:15:53.733
But did you prepare all of this?

763
01:15:53.800 --> 01:15:56.267
Are you questioning me? You?

764
01:15:58.400 --> 01:16:00.533
A traveler dying of hunger.

765
01:16:00.600 --> 01:16:02.267
who hasn't touched the food.

766
01:16:03.000 --> 01:16:06.533
He claims to be a sailor. but
he wears the armor of a great warrior.

767
01:16:07.467 --> 01:16:09.800
And it violates the Law of Zeus

768
01:16:09.867 --> 01:16:12.800
by carrying a dagger into his landlady's home.

769
01:16:12.867 --> 01:16:14.400
You have a keen eye.

770
01:16:14.467 --> 01:16:16.333
Sharpened by the gods.

771
01:16:16.867 --> 01:16:18.867
Do you have visions?

772
01:16:18.933 --> 01:16:20.267
Truths.

773
01:16:21.400 --> 01:16:22.867
Sometimes.

774
01:16:22.933 --> 01:16:25.067
Would they tell you where my people are?

775
01:16:25.933 --> 01:16:27.133
Your people?

776
01:16:27.200 --> 01:16:28.333
That's right.

777
01:16:28.867 --> 01:16:30.267
They would do it.

778
01:16:30.333 --> 01:16:31.533
Would you tell me?

779
01:16:34.933 --> 01:16:36.667
If you give me your knife.

780
01:16:45.200 --> 01:16:48.400
Why should I help a liar who
rejects my hospitality?

781
01:16:59.400 --> 01:17:02.267
Your people were here. but now they're gone.

782
01:17:08.867 --> 01:17:09.933
Did they move forward. for example?

783
01:17:14.400 --> 01:17:15.733
And did they leave their rings behind?

784
01:17:23.733 --> 01:17:24.733
I wouldn't do that.

785
01:17:26.533 --> 01:17:27.533
No!

786
01:17:27.600 --> 01:17:29.333
I don't know who the raven is.

787
01:17:29.400 --> 01:17:32.467
But if you don't let him go. I'll
strangle him. and he'll find out.

788
01:17:32.533 --> 01:17:33.867
Don't hurt her.

789
01:17:33.933 --> 01:17:35.067
Is he/she related to you?

790
01:17:36.867 --> 01:17:38.267
Your mom?

791
01:17:40.467 --> 01:17:41.467
Your sister.

792
01:17:45.067 --> 01:17:47.933
He turned her into one. just as
he turned my people into pigs.

793
01:17:48.000 --> 01:17:49.467
I haven't turned them into anything.

794
01:17:50.333 --> 01:17:53.267
Look at them. That's just how they are. Look at them!

795
01:17:53.333 --> 01:17:56.067
Despicable. Disgusting.

796
01:17:56.133 --> 01:17:58.667
With base instincts. valued in battle.

797
01:17:58.733 --> 01:18:01.467
They raped and looted
everywhere because of you.

798
01:18:01.533 --> 01:18:03.267
Do you think they're acting
against their nature?

799
01:18:03.333 --> 01:18:05.933
Do they do that just because you tell them to?

800
01:18:06.000 --> 01:18:09.600
A true commander believes
that brutality is discipline.

801
01:18:10.400 --> 01:18:11.800
But you don't seem naive.

802
01:18:12.533 --> 01:18:14.467
But you sent them to me.

803
01:18:14.533 --> 01:18:15.933
At my place. by herself.

804
01:18:16.000 --> 01:18:19.400
Some filthy soldiers. with
empty stomachs and hot blood.

805
01:18:21.933 --> 01:18:24.667
Did you think they would respect me in my own home?

806
01:18:25.400 --> 01:18:26.800
Look at them!

807
01:18:26.867 --> 01:18:28.000
Look at them.

808
01:18:29.667 --> 01:18:31.000
Did they hurt you?

809
01:18:31.067 --> 01:18:32.467
They were going to do it.

810
01:18:33.267 --> 01:18:34.733
They just want to go home.

811
01:18:38.133 --> 01:18:39.400
Leave them alone.

812
01:19:05.467 --> 01:19:06.800
Come back.

813
01:19:07.800 --> 01:19:09.400
Come back.

814
01:19:10.000 --> 01:19:11.533
Come back.

815
01:19:11.600 --> 01:19:13.600
Go back to your costumes.

816
01:19:17.867 --> 01:19:19.400
Come back.

817
01:19:19.467 --> 01:19:21.733
Go back to your disguises.

818
01:19:21.800 --> 01:19:23.600
Come back.

819
01:19:23.667 --> 01:19:25.800
Go back to your disguises.

820
01:19:25.867 --> 01:19:28.400
Come back. Come back.

821
01:19:29.467 --> 01:19:31.133
Come back.

822
01:19:31.200 --> 01:19:33.333
Go back to your disguises.

823
01:19:33.400 --> 01:19:34.533
Come back.

824
01:19:38.800 --> 01:19:40.400
Odysseus.

825
01:19:40.467 --> 01:19:41.800
Go back to the ship.

826
01:19:41.867 --> 01:19:43.600
Take as much food as you can.

827
01:19:44.200 --> 01:19:45.467
Leave the meat.

828
01:19:45.533 --> 01:19:48.267
You can't let her live. 
She's a witch.

829
01:19:48.333 --> 01:19:49.533
Who gave us strength?

830
01:19:49.600 --> 01:19:51.600
Your gods.

831
01:19:51.667 --> 01:19:53.133
Fill the bladders from the stream.

832
01:19:53.200 --> 01:19:54.667
We set sail at dawn.

833
01:20:00.400 --> 01:20:02.467
Why did you leave her like that?

834
01:20:02.533 --> 01:20:04.000
That way. we understand each other better.

835
01:20:05.667 --> 01:20:07.667
I'm sorry if we offended you.

836
01:20:08.733 --> 01:20:11.000
We can't go back 
without your help.

837
01:20:11.067 --> 01:20:13.067
To reach 
the trade routes.

838
01:20:13.133 --> 01:20:14.733
You've really lost your way.

839
01:20:14.800 --> 01:20:15.867
What?

840
01:20:17.467 --> 01:20:20.267
Only the blind Tiresias 
can show you the way.

841
01:20:20.333 --> 01:20:21.400
Tiresias?

842
01:20:21.467 --> 01:20:23.000
In Hades.

843
01:20:23.067 --> 01:20:25.067
No one swims through 
the Underworld.

844
01:20:25.133 --> 01:20:26.267
And he returns.

845
01:20:26.333 --> 01:20:27.400
You'll come back.

846
01:20:28.667 --> 01:20:30.667
Go north. 
Toward the sunset.

847
01:20:36.467 --> 01:20:38.400
And he went further inland.

848
01:20:38.467 --> 01:20:41.267
Where the river of fire
meets the river of ice…

849
01:21:31.933 --> 01:21:33.467
Dig a trench.

850
01:21:36.600 --> 01:21:38.600
Fill it with 
sacrificial blood.

851
01:21:59.000 --> 01:22:00.267
Gather the dead.

852
01:22:01.467 --> 01:22:03.267
And figure out how to 
get back home.

853
01:22:05.333 --> 01:22:06.600
You.

854
01:22:07.667 --> 01:22:09.267
Not your people.

855
01:22:09.333 --> 01:22:10.867
Did you curse them?

856
01:22:11.400 --> 01:22:12.667
It's not a curse.

857
01:22:13.600 --> 01:22:14.867
It's true.

858
01:22:15.933 --> 01:22:18.467
They don't want you to 
provoke the gods.

859
01:22:20.333 --> 01:22:22.600
The gods say that
only you will return.

860
01:22:23.667 --> 01:22:25.467
Then I challenge them.

861
01:23:06.633 --> 01:23:07.633
Synonym.

862
01:23:08.833 --> 01:23:09.900
I am Odysseus.

863
01:23:14.767 --> 01:23:15.767
Synonym.

864
01:23:16.967 --> 01:23:17.967
I am Odysseus.

865
01:23:20.700 --> 01:23:22.367
Let him drink. Odysseus.

866
01:23:23.167 --> 01:23:25.033
Then you'll hear 
his confession.

867
01:23:39.500 --> 01:23:40.700
Odysseus?

868
01:23:42.167 --> 01:23:44.300
He fooled everyone.

869
01:23:47.767 --> 01:23:48.967
And why me?

870
01:23:50.767 --> 01:23:52.433
I needed you to 
believe me.

871
01:23:54.367 --> 01:23:56.567
Why was it proposed?

872
01:23:56.633 --> 01:23:58.433
Don't you remember 
how I got here?

873
01:23:58.500 --> 01:24:01.433
I asked you to take 
Antinous's place.

874
01:24:01.500 --> 01:24:03.567
It fell on him.

875
01:24:03.633 --> 01:24:06.367
Offer money to
your family for your spot.

876
01:24:07.500 --> 01:24:09.633
If the Trojans 
don't let me live…

877
01:24:11.567 --> 01:24:13.567
If I never 
come home.

878
01:24:13.633 --> 01:24:15.767
Take this to my father.

879
01:24:16.833 --> 01:24:18.300
Tell him everything 
I did.

880
01:24:21.633 --> 01:24:23.033
Odysseus.

881
01:24:24.367 --> 01:24:26.833
Feel free to
add something of your own.

882
01:24:30.033 --> 01:24:32.433
Couldn't you have 
told me the truth?

883
01:24:34.167 --> 01:24:36.300
You knew I would die for you.

884
01:24:36.367 --> 01:24:39.233
The dying man confesses. 
You should have believed him.

885
01:24:39.300 --> 01:24:42.100
I died for you on that beach.

886
01:24:42.833 --> 01:24:44.967
I know. I was there with you.

887
01:24:45.500 --> 01:24:48.433
I heard you repeating
that lie with your last breath.

888
01:24:48.500 --> 01:24:50.167
And it brought us victory.

889
01:24:50.233 --> 01:24:52.833
I was there. Right there.
Smelling your blood.

890
01:24:52.900 --> 01:24:56.367
What comfort 
does this bring me?

891
01:24:56.433 --> 01:25:00.367
Without knowing you were inside.
until the dead told me.

892
01:25:01.300 --> 01:25:02.767
Which dead people?

893
01:25:02.833 --> 01:25:06.100
Many veterans of your
campaigns wander along these beaches.

894
01:25:08.500 --> 01:25:13.300
The dead come to mourn the living
and to welcome the newly dead.

895
01:25:14.300 --> 01:25:16.233
My father found me first.

896
01:25:16.900 --> 01:25:18.233
To comfort you.

897
01:25:18.300 --> 01:25:19.967
Shadows bring no comfort.

898
01:25:20.500 --> 01:25:23.833
They want news about the living
who have offended them.

899
01:25:24.633 --> 01:25:27.367
My father had died in poverty.

900
01:25:27.433 --> 01:25:32.233
alone. sifting through 
people like Antinous.

901
01:25:32.300 --> 01:25:34.100
Did Antinous deceive you?

902
01:25:34.700 --> 01:25:37.833
And she's definitely tricking you. too.
- How?

903
01:25:37.900 --> 01:25:40.500
Who is taking care of 
your wife and son in Ithaca?

904
01:25:40.567 --> 01:25:42.433
My wife isn't a problem.

905
01:25:44.033 --> 01:25:46.700
Talk to Agamemnon 
about his return.

906
01:25:46.767 --> 01:25:48.633
Is Agamemnon here?

907
01:25:51.967 --> 01:25:54.300
You buried me with 
honors in Troy.

908
01:25:54.367 --> 01:25:56.167
Thank you.

909
01:25:56.233 --> 01:25:58.033
But think about those 
who weren't there.

910
01:25:58.567 --> 01:26:00.967
It's not always possible.

911
01:26:01.033 --> 01:26:04.300
When you get home. 
take a ship.

912
01:26:04.367 --> 01:26:07.567
Set sail for the unknown
West until you discover land.

913
01:26:07.633 --> 01:26:09.833
And there. offer a sacrifice

914
01:26:09.900 --> 01:26:11.900
and honor your people.

915
01:26:13.433 --> 01:26:15.100
Do you still have that tree?

916
01:26:18.767 --> 01:26:19.767
Of course.

917
01:26:20.567 --> 01:26:21.633
If you manage to get home.

918
01:26:21.700 --> 01:26:23.833
Restore Antinous's shame.

919
01:26:23.900 --> 01:26:24.900
I'll do that.

920
01:26:36.233 --> 01:26:37.300
My lord.

921
01:26:48.433 --> 01:26:49.433
Odysseus.

922
01:26:50.767 --> 01:26:52.300
Why are you here?

923
01:26:52.367 --> 01:26:54.433
We won the war. 
You were on your way home.

924
01:26:55.167 --> 01:26:58.700
Odysseus. the most brilliant
of all my generals.

925
01:27:00.100 --> 01:27:01.700
I hope you don't end up like me.

926
01:27:01.767 --> 01:27:03.700
Have you. too. been left 
to fend for yourselves like us?

927
01:27:05.433 --> 01:27:07.067
My misfortune was 
to return home.

928
01:27:08.567 --> 01:27:10.567
To be greeted by 
my beautiful wife.

929
01:27:16.767 --> 01:27:17.767
I want to take a bath.

930
01:27:20.433 --> 01:27:21.433
Anointed.

931
01:27:23.900 --> 01:27:24.900
Reassured.

932
01:27:26.367 --> 01:27:27.733
To learn the hardest lesson.

933
01:27:29.033 --> 01:27:31.367
"Had I poisoned her heart?"

934
01:27:34.033 --> 01:27:36.033
Which already belonged to someone else.

935
01:27:38.700 --> 01:27:41.167
No! No.

936
01:27:41.900 --> 01:27:44.567
She and her lover?
They had been planning my death for years.

937
01:27:46.233 --> 01:27:47.233
Odysseus.

938
01:27:49.700 --> 01:27:53.033
My son. Orestes. 
did you avenge me?

939
01:27:53.100 --> 01:27:54.433
My lord. how am I supposed to know?

940
01:27:55.567 --> 01:27:57.233
I'm still trying to get home.

941
01:27:58.233 --> 01:27:59.233
Don't make the same mistake I did.

942
01:28:00.900 --> 01:28:03.900
Don't cross the threshold.
expecting wreaths and praise.

943
01:28:04.767 --> 01:28:06.700
Go there after you've changed.

944
01:28:06.767 --> 01:28:08.033
Don't rush.

945
01:28:08.767 --> 01:28:09.767
Analyze.

946
01:28:17.033 --> 01:28:18.100
Do you see?

947
01:28:20.100 --> 01:28:21.500
I know what I need to do.

948
01:28:21.567 --> 01:28:23.500
Partly.

949
01:28:23.567 --> 01:28:26.033
My prophecy is not meant for their ears.

950
01:28:27.167 --> 01:28:28.167
Go to the ship.

951
01:28:30.500 --> 01:28:31.700
Go southwest.

952
01:28:31.767 --> 01:28:33.433
Beyond the sirens.

953
01:28:34.367 --> 01:28:36.767
They lure men to the cliffs.

954
01:28:36.833 --> 01:28:37.967
Everyone dies.

955
01:28:38.033 --> 01:28:39.767
I won't let that happen.

956
01:28:39.833 --> 01:28:42.300
I'm going to plug their ears. With wax.

957
01:28:42.367 --> 01:28:43.567
After the sirens

958
01:28:43.633 --> 01:28:46.100
You choose between Charybdis.
the whirlpool.

959
01:28:46.167 --> 01:28:47.633
and the monster Scylla.

960
01:28:47.700 --> 01:28:50.433
The whirlpool in the sea
takes everyone's life.

961
01:28:50.500 --> 01:28:52.767
The Monster—Only Six of Them.

962
01:28:52.833 --> 01:28:56.900
But your determination not to sacrifice
any more lives is killing them all.

963
01:28:58.233 --> 01:28:59.300
I'll do what needs to be done.

964
01:28:59.367 --> 01:29:02.100
Finally. when 
famine and rebellion threaten.

965
01:29:02.167 --> 01:29:05.567
They lead you to the shore. where
the Sun God's cattle graze.

966
01:29:06.500 --> 01:29:08.033
They sacrifice 
Apollo's cattle.

967
01:29:09.367 --> 01:29:11.167
Zeus kills them because of this.

968
01:29:11.233 --> 01:29:12.367
Do you see?

969
01:29:13.433 --> 01:29:14.767
I'll stop them.

970
01:29:14.833 --> 01:29:18.367
Why do you value their lives more than
those of the ones you've already lost?

971
01:29:18.433 --> 01:29:20.367
I can save them from the gods.

972
01:29:21.567 --> 01:29:23.367
But not from them themselves.

973
01:29:25.833 --> 01:29:26.900
What is this?

974
01:29:26.967 --> 01:29:28.567
Those he did not bury.

975
01:29:32.033 --> 01:29:34.167
Who demand that you 
share in their shame.

976
01:29:36.300 --> 01:29:37.700
Let's go!

977
01:29:52.367 --> 01:29:54.300
Let go of the ropes!

978
01:29:55.233 --> 01:29:57.300
We have to set sail!

979
01:30:22.300 --> 01:30:23.433
Is this our fate?

980
01:30:26.167 --> 01:30:27.967
Is this our fate?

981
01:30:48.500 --> 01:30:50.700
We rowed from the underworld.

982
01:30:51.900 --> 01:30:54.300
leaving so many comrades behind.

983
01:30:55.100 --> 01:30:56.700
I have to save the others.

984
01:30:57.567 --> 01:30:58.633
What happened to them?

985
01:30:59.567 --> 01:31:01.100
Did they leave me here?

986
01:31:01.167 --> 01:31:02.633
Will they come back?

987
01:31:04.833 --> 01:31:06.167
Did you do something to them?

988
01:31:08.033 --> 01:31:09.567
Did you do something to them?

989
01:31:09.633 --> 01:31:10.633
Take some.

990
01:31:11.300 --> 01:31:13.567
She told me not to eat the lotus.

991
01:31:13.633 --> 01:31:14.700
Just tonight.

992
01:31:15.500 --> 01:31:17.167
So you can get some sleep.

993
01:31:20.433 --> 01:31:24.100
And tomorrow. when you're ready. 
you'll remember.

994
01:31:26.767 --> 01:31:28.900
You'll remember your crew.

995
01:31:28.967 --> 01:31:30.100
And for his family.

996
01:31:30.167 --> 01:31:31.167
Family.

997
01:31:33.433 --> 01:31:34.500
Wife?

998
01:31:36.367 --> 01:31:37.767
Do I have a spouse?

999
01:31:42.300 --> 01:31:43.433
What are you doing?

1000
01:31:43.500 --> 01:31:45.433
Everything's fine. Melanto.

1001
01:31:45.500 --> 01:31:47.033
No strangers allowed.

1002
01:31:47.100 --> 01:31:48.433
With or without the curtain.

1003
01:31:48.500 --> 01:31:51.567
After three years of feasting.
we're no strangers. Penelope.

1004
01:31:51.633 --> 01:31:54.967
Do you want to marry me
or ruin your reputation?

1005
01:31:55.033 --> 01:31:56.233
Melanto won't speak.

1006
01:31:56.833 --> 01:32:00.233
Actually. I came because
I'm worried about your reputation.

1007
01:32:01.633 --> 01:32:03.233
Everyone can see how hard you work.

1008
01:32:03.300 --> 01:32:05.767
Slow but steady progress.

1009
01:32:06.967 --> 01:32:09.500
But every night you undo what you've done.

1010
01:32:15.167 --> 01:32:16.367
Leave us alone.

1011
01:32:18.033 --> 01:32:21.833
Don't expect him to be loyal to
a master he has never met.

1012
01:32:21.900 --> 01:32:23.767
I expect him to be loyal to me.

1013
01:32:23.833 --> 01:32:24.900
He will be loyal.

1014
01:32:24.967 --> 01:32:27.433
When you marry me.

1015
01:32:27.500 --> 01:32:31.167
Slaves can be
loyal only to the future…

1016
01:32:31.233 --> 01:32:33.500
And this house is waiting for an owner.

1017
01:32:33.567 --> 01:32:35.967
And the kingdom of Ithaca is waiting for a king.

1018
01:32:38.433 --> 01:32:40.367
The King of Ithaca will return.

1019
01:32:40.433 --> 01:32:41.633
No. There isn't.

1020
01:32:43.433 --> 01:32:44.833
Did you know that when I was young

1021
01:32:44.900 --> 01:32:46.300
Did I try to go with him to Troy?

1022
01:32:47.433 --> 01:32:48.767
He never told me.

1023
01:32:50.233 --> 01:32:51.700
He refused to let me go.

1024
01:32:51.767 --> 01:32:55.033
He knew that Ithaca. without him.
would need a future.

1025
01:33:01.833 --> 01:33:03.900
And he saw me as part of that future.

1026
01:33:08.567 --> 01:33:13.433
I will never tell
the grooms about your deception.

1027
01:33:16.233 --> 01:33:17.567
Finish the weaving.

1028
01:33:19.567 --> 01:33:20.900
You have to let him go now.

1029
01:33:30.367 --> 01:33:32.833
Why would you marry
someone who loves someone else?

1030
01:33:35.833 --> 01:33:40.633
Your loyalty to Odysseus is one
of your greatest virtues.

1031
01:33:43.967 --> 01:33:45.833
I love you.

1032
01:33:45.900 --> 01:33:49.033
I want you to choose me and
put an end to all of this.

1033
01:33:50.700 --> 01:33:53.833
Don't tell me you're not tired.

1034
01:33:53.900 --> 01:33:55.233
From waiting.

1035
01:33:55.300 --> 01:33:56.567
Because of the uncertainty.

1036
01:33:58.633 --> 01:34:01.233
He has already fulfilled his duty…

1037
01:34:02.433 --> 01:34:05.967
Year after year after year.

1038
01:34:08.900 --> 01:34:10.433
You need to think about yourself.

1039
01:34:12.300 --> 01:34:13.633
It's time to live.

1040
01:34:19.233 --> 01:34:20.833
Odysseus would have wanted that.

1041
01:34:27.767 --> 01:34:29.300
I'll give you whatever you need.

1042
01:34:33.833 --> 01:34:34.967
My queen.

1043
01:34:38.167 --> 01:34:39.367
And what about my son?

1044
01:34:40.900 --> 01:34:42.500
The throne is his.

1045
01:34:42.567 --> 01:34:43.567
He is the heir.

1046
01:34:46.033 --> 01:34:48.433
Unless we have a son.

1047
01:34:58.500 --> 01:34:59.833
Where is Telemachus?

1048
01:35:01.500 --> 01:35:03.100
He went inland.

1049
01:35:03.167 --> 01:35:04.767
To see his grandfather.

1050
01:35:12.767 --> 01:35:14.300
He's lying.

1051
01:35:14.367 --> 01:35:15.900
He set sail on the sea.

1052
01:35:30.167 --> 01:35:32.033
He went to find out about his father.

1053
01:35:32.700 --> 01:35:33.833
Where?

1054
01:35:33.900 --> 01:35:35.100
In Sparta.

1055
01:35:35.167 --> 01:35:36.233
At Menelaus's.

1056
01:35:37.500 --> 01:35:38.567
Are we going?

1057
01:35:40.100 --> 01:35:41.100
No.

1058
01:35:42.233 --> 01:35:45.433
You're always so loud.
Melantiy. for a cowherd!

1059
01:35:46.633 --> 01:35:48.300
You were eavesdropping.
- No. no.

1060
01:35:48.367 --> 01:35:49.367
No. No.

1061
01:35:51.567 --> 01:35:52.767
I came for Argos.

1062
01:35:54.300 --> 01:35:56.833
The dog is staying there. Go
back to the farm.

1063
01:35:57.633 --> 01:35:59.900
We'll send Melantius
to pick up your pigs.

1064
01:35:59.967 --> 01:36:01.633
You're no longer welcome here. 
old man.

1065
01:36:02.367 --> 01:36:03.633
I obey only 
my mistress.

1066
01:36:03.700 --> 01:36:04.767
Does she even know your name?

1067
01:36:09.633 --> 01:36:10.967
Go away.

1068
01:36:11.033 --> 01:36:12.600
Telemachus will return 
through the temple in Pylos.

1069
01:36:13.833 --> 01:36:15.400
Wait for him there with your people.

1070
01:36:17.300 --> 01:36:18.667
And you're left to 
woo the queen?

1071
01:36:19.367 --> 01:36:20.367
No way.

1072
01:36:21.367 --> 01:36:23.167
He must die along the way.

1073
01:36:23.233 --> 01:36:24.833
Iro has a plan.

1074
01:36:29.033 --> 01:36:32.967
I want him and his men dead.
In a ditch. With their throats slit.

1075
01:36:33.033 --> 01:36:35.367
Telemachus and his men will
be on the lookout for bandits

1076
01:36:35.433 --> 01:36:38.567
on the way from here to Pylos.

1077
01:36:38.633 --> 01:36:42.167
But if we first take the temple away from the priests…

1078
01:36:43.567 --> 01:36:45.567
And bring me something
from him as proof.

1079
01:36:47.100 --> 01:36:48.167
His nose.

1080
01:36:48.833 --> 01:36:50.567
Is that how you'll recognize him?

1081
01:36:54.033 --> 01:36:55.233
And his ears. too.

1082
01:37:00.700 --> 01:37:01.767
Who taught you that?

1083
01:37:02.367 --> 01:37:03.900
My father's pig farmer.

1084
01:37:03.967 --> 01:37:05.100
The Pig Farmer.

1085
01:37:05.167 --> 01:37:06.567
Yes. his most loyal servant.

1086
01:37:07.300 --> 01:37:10.300
Odysseus left behind his most loyal servant.

1087
01:37:10.367 --> 01:37:11.833
He's always been the smartest.

1088
01:37:12.833 --> 01:37:14.833
The only one 
Agamemnon would listen to.

1089
01:37:16.100 --> 01:37:17.300
She should have listened to him more.

1090
01:37:18.033 --> 01:37:19.633
So what happened to your brother?

1091
01:37:19.700 --> 01:37:20.967
His wife betrayed him.

1092
01:37:21.033 --> 01:37:22.033
He killed him.

1093
01:37:22.833 --> 01:37:23.967
What did you do to them?

1094
01:37:24.033 --> 01:37:26.033
His son. Orestes. killed them both.

1095
01:37:26.833 --> 01:37:28.300
What? His own mother?

1096
01:37:29.567 --> 01:37:30.633
Avenge your father.

1097
01:37:31.700 --> 01:37:33.233
You don't need to worry.

1098
01:37:33.300 --> 01:37:35.900
Your mother isn't like Clytemnestra.

1099
01:37:35.967 --> 01:37:37.100
That might be the case.

1100
01:37:37.167 --> 01:37:40.100
The grooms convinced the elders
that she should get married.

1101
01:37:41.167 --> 01:37:43.567
This is no time for an empty throne.

1102
01:37:43.633 --> 01:37:45.900
You know how it is. The maritime nations.

1103
01:37:45.967 --> 01:37:47.100
Were they attacked?

1104
01:37:47.167 --> 01:37:49.700
No. but the attacks 
seem to be getting closer.

1105
01:37:49.767 --> 01:37:51.100
Sparta is ready.

1106
01:37:51.167 --> 01:37:52.700
But what about Ithaca?

1107
01:37:52.767 --> 01:37:55.100
He must gather an army 
to defend himself.

1108
01:37:55.167 --> 01:37:56.633
Someone has to take the throne.

1109
01:37:56.700 --> 01:37:59.033
The battle is over there. not here. son.

1110
01:37:59.833 --> 01:38:01.433
He ran away from the problem.

1111
01:38:02.367 --> 01:38:04.833
He left his mother with the grooms.

1112
01:38:06.167 --> 01:38:08.100
Should I hire mercenaries?

1113
01:38:08.167 --> 01:38:09.500
To kill them all?

1114
01:38:10.300 --> 01:38:12.633
To kill wealthy and powerful
guests in your own home?

1115
01:38:13.167 --> 01:38:14.433
They'll kick you out.

1116
01:38:15.300 --> 01:38:16.433
So what?

1117
01:38:16.500 --> 01:38:19.300
You must drive them away without
breaking the Law of Zeus.

1118
01:38:21.433 --> 01:38:23.100
I must take the throne.

1119
01:38:24.433 --> 01:38:26.100
I need to know if 
Odysseus is alive.

1120
01:38:26.700 --> 01:38:30.100
This has nothing to do with a father
you've never known.

1121
01:38:30.167 --> 01:38:31.900
You need to take control.

1122
01:38:31.967 --> 01:38:33.633
Regardless of Odysseus.

1123
01:38:47.767 --> 01:38:49.300
You have to remember.

1124
01:38:49.367 --> 01:38:50.367
No. that's not true.

1125
01:38:51.300 --> 01:38:52.500
I don't need to 
know the truth.

1126
01:38:52.567 --> 01:38:54.233
I'm happy here. With you.

1127
01:38:56.433 --> 01:39:00.233
Then why are you gathering
the wreckage of your ship to make a raft?

1128
01:39:02.900 --> 01:39:04.700
Remember your crew.

1129
01:39:05.900 --> 01:39:06.900
His trip.

1130
01:39:11.233 --> 01:39:13.300
The Song of the Sirens.

1131
01:39:22.967 --> 01:39:24.700
Put wax in their ears.

1132
01:39:25.767 --> 01:39:27.700
Not me. Tie me to the mast.

1133
01:39:27.767 --> 01:39:28.567
Why?

1134
01:39:28.633 --> 01:39:30.167
I want to hear the song of the sirens.

1135
01:39:30.233 --> 01:39:31.233
No.

1136
01:39:32.100 --> 01:39:33.433
That's a bad idea.

1137
01:39:33.500 --> 01:39:35.033
No one survived.

1138
01:39:35.100 --> 01:39:37.233
That's exactly why. 
I'll be the first.

1139
01:39:38.300 --> 01:39:41.167
Even if I beg. 
don't untie me

1140
01:39:41.233 --> 01:39:42.233
until we get through it.

1141
01:39:43.433 --> 01:39:44.433
Check their ears.

1142
01:39:45.633 --> 01:39:48.567
Extra rations for
anyone who says. “Yes. sir!”

1143
01:40:46.967 --> 01:40:48.433
What is the song of the sirens?

1144
01:40:49.700 --> 01:40:51.900
It is everything 
you want it to be.

1145
01:40:52.567 --> 01:40:56.100
And then—everything you
wish you hadn't wished for.

1146
01:41:14.233 --> 01:41:17.433
That sweet itch. 
the one you just want to scratch.

1147
01:41:18.433 --> 01:41:19.833
but you can feel it deep down.

1148
01:41:19.900 --> 01:41:21.167
You can't catch up to him.

1149
01:41:23.567 --> 01:41:25.900
Although it's sweet. 
it becomes unbearable.

1150
01:41:30.433 --> 01:41:32.233
It tells you that what 
you want most.

1151
01:41:34.500 --> 01:41:36.367
That's exactly 
what you won't have.

1152
01:41:40.033 --> 01:41:41.900
And what you 
can no longer have.

1153
01:41:43.233 --> 01:41:45.500
It's what you already had…

1154
01:41:45.567 --> 01:41:46.767
And you lost.

1155
01:41:55.300 --> 01:41:57.833
It was the song of the promises
I didn't keep.

1156
01:42:03.100 --> 01:42:05.367
And you said that I didn't want
to go back home.

1157
01:42:08.567 --> 01:42:09.833
Foam in the water!

1158
01:42:10.567 --> 01:42:11.700
Foam ahead!

1159
01:42:14.633 --> 01:42:15.700
Charybdis.

1160
01:42:17.033 --> 01:42:18.367
The Monstrous Whirlpool.

1161
01:42:21.167 --> 01:42:22.167
Hoist the sails!

1162
01:42:22.700 --> 01:42:23.967
Come on. hurry up!

1163
01:42:24.033 --> 01:42:26.100
Weigh anchor!

1164
01:42:26.167 --> 01:42:27.567
Are we going out to the open sea?

1165
01:42:27.633 --> 01:42:28.633
He's already caught us.

1166
01:42:30.900 --> 01:42:32.300
Let's go!

1167
01:42:32.367 --> 01:42:34.167
To the north! Get moving!

1168
01:42:35.367 --> 01:42:36.500
Toward the canyon.

1169
01:42:36.567 --> 01:42:37.967
It's very cramped.

1170
01:42:38.033 --> 01:42:39.567
He headed toward the whirlpool.

1171
01:42:39.633 --> 01:42:41.233
If you're walking along the edge.

1172
01:42:41.300 --> 01:42:43.700
It might take us to the other side.

1173
01:42:43.767 --> 01:42:45.300
Don't worry. We'll manage.

1174
01:42:50.567 --> 01:42:52.767
No. No!

1175
01:42:52.833 --> 01:42:54.100
We're heading to the canal.

1176
01:42:54.167 --> 01:42:56.100
Take it easy.
- To the canal!

1177
01:42:56.167 --> 01:42:57.167
Paddles inside!

1178
01:42:59.100 --> 01:43:00.300
Put away the oars!

1179
01:43:00.833 --> 01:43:03.700
Right oars. 
get ready to stop!

1180
01:43:10.367 --> 01:43:11.633
On my signal!

1181
01:43:13.033 --> 01:43:14.033
Three.

1182
01:43:16.167 --> 01:43:17.167
two.

1183
01:43:18.233 --> 01:43:19.300
One!

1184
01:43:19.367 --> 01:43:20.367
Hi!

1185
01:43:21.500 --> 01:43:22.833
Pull out the oars!

1186
01:43:33.900 --> 01:43:35.033
Hold on!

1187
01:44:08.567 --> 01:44:10.167
We did it!

1188
01:44:10.233 --> 01:44:11.233
We did it!

1189
01:44:12.100 --> 01:44:14.500
We did it.
The gods are with us…

1190
01:44:17.700 --> 01:44:18.700
Row!

1191
01:44:20.100 --> 01:44:21.167
Row!

1192
01:44:21.833 --> 01:44:23.367
Row!

1193
01:44:23.433 --> 01:44:24.767
Row!

1194
01:44:24.833 --> 01:44:26.500
Stay the course!

1195
01:44:28.833 --> 01:44:30.233
Row!

1196
01:44:32.300 --> 01:44:33.767
Row!

1197
01:44:33.833 --> 01:44:35.167
Stay the course!

1198
01:44:35.233 --> 01:44:38.500
Pull!

1199
01:44:40.967 --> 01:44:41.967
Pull!

1200
01:45:01.967 --> 01:45:02.967
You already knew that.

1201
01:45:03.633 --> 01:45:04.633
And he didn't tell us.

1202
01:45:05.300 --> 01:45:07.767
We weren't going to go through here.

1203
01:45:07.833 --> 01:45:09.767
And those men would still be alive!

1204
01:45:09.833 --> 01:45:10.967
No!

1205
01:45:11.033 --> 01:45:12.367
We were all going to die.

1206
01:45:12.433 --> 01:45:14.033
You had no right!

1207
01:45:14.100 --> 01:45:15.433
I had every right.

1208
01:45:15.500 --> 01:45:17.100
And all the responsibility!

1209
01:45:17.167 --> 01:45:20.233
The whirlpool was going to 
destroy us all.

1210
01:45:20.300 --> 01:45:21.433
That's how we live.

1211
01:45:21.500 --> 01:45:22.700
Just a few!

1212
01:45:22.767 --> 01:45:23.767
Yes!

1213
01:45:27.167 --> 01:45:28.767
What else did Tiresias tell you?

1214
01:45:40.833 --> 01:45:41.833
I'll dock there.

1215
01:45:44.633 --> 01:45:47.900
You can't go there. That is
the island of the Sun God.

1216
01:45:47.967 --> 01:45:49.233
We need to repair the ship.

1217
01:45:50.633 --> 01:45:52.367
They're exhausted!

1218
01:45:52.433 --> 01:45:53.833
We lost six!

1219
01:45:53.900 --> 01:45:55.367
We're about to dock.

1220
01:45:55.433 --> 01:45:57.100
We'll take a break there.

1221
01:45:57.167 --> 01:45:58.433
Everyone is going to die!

1222
01:46:01.500 --> 01:46:03.633
Did you ask what 
Tiresias told me?

1223
01:46:04.567 --> 01:46:06.500
That everyone will die.

1224
01:46:06.567 --> 01:46:09.900
If they sacrifice
the livestock to the Sun God.

1225
01:46:09.967 --> 01:46:12.033
who lives on this island.

1226
01:46:12.100 --> 01:46:13.100
Everyone dies.

1227
01:46:17.300 --> 01:46:18.300
Okay.

1228
01:46:19.300 --> 01:46:20.300
We're about to dock.

1229
01:46:21.167 --> 01:46:22.567
We'll cook our own meal.

1230
01:46:22.633 --> 01:46:24.500
We're getting some fruit. Water.

1231
01:46:24.567 --> 01:46:25.700
Nothing more.

1232
01:46:26.967 --> 01:46:28.633
Swear to it.

1233
01:46:28.700 --> 01:46:29.967
Everyone.

1234
01:46:43.900 --> 01:46:44.900
Do you see?

1235
01:46:45.767 --> 01:46:47.300
The wind brought us here.

1236
01:46:48.100 --> 01:46:49.100
We took a break.

1237
01:46:50.100 --> 01:46:51.367
We repaired the ship.

1238
01:46:51.433 --> 01:46:53.767
And we'll set sail 
as soon as the wind changes.

1239
01:46:54.567 --> 01:46:55.633
Grab that rope.

1240
01:47:06.500 --> 01:47:08.500
Did you keep your promise?

1241
01:47:09.700 --> 01:47:10.700
Yes.

1242
01:47:11.433 --> 01:47:12.633
Quite a long time.

1243
01:47:14.367 --> 01:47:16.033
But the wind didn't change.

1244
01:47:16.100 --> 01:47:20.100
The steady breeze that
allowed us to dock made

1245
01:47:22.500 --> 01:47:24.100
It's impossible for us to set sail.

1246
01:47:28.433 --> 01:47:29.900
We're out of food.

1247
01:47:33.233 --> 01:47:34.233
Go away.

1248
01:48:36.833 --> 01:48:40.233
Did you think that if you ate in
silence. God wouldn't hear you?

1249
01:48:40.300 --> 01:48:41.833
We thought you wouldn't hear us.

1250
01:48:42.633 --> 01:48:44.167
I'm not the problem.

1251
01:48:44.233 --> 01:48:45.500
I wanted them alive.

1252
01:48:51.167 --> 01:48:52.633
Tiresias told you that.

1253
01:48:52.700 --> 01:48:54.900
Circe. The gods told you so.

1254
01:48:55.767 --> 01:48:58.167
Gods do not speak in order to be understood.

1255
01:48:58.233 --> 01:48:59.833
Isn't the pain obvious?

1256
01:49:00.833 --> 01:49:02.367
Blood or death?

1257
01:49:03.033 --> 01:49:04.700
When you watch them die.
do you see a coincidence?

1258
01:49:04.767 --> 01:49:06.633
or consequences?

1259
01:49:06.700 --> 01:49:09.233
I see the consequences. 
I see my failure.

1260
01:49:09.833 --> 01:49:12.567
What if their death is
their failure. not yours?

1261
01:49:12.633 --> 01:49:13.700
I'm leading them.

1262
01:49:15.300 --> 01:49:16.433
I brought them here.

1263
01:49:17.033 --> 01:49:18.167
And who about you?

1264
01:49:19.500 --> 01:49:20.833
Are you greater than God?

1265
01:49:20.900 --> 01:49:23.100
Why are you taking responsibility?

1266
01:49:23.900 --> 01:49:25.633
Why don't you want to come back?

1267
01:49:25.700 --> 01:49:27.167
What were you expecting?

1268
01:49:28.767 --> 01:49:30.233
They ran out of food.

1269
01:49:31.233 --> 01:49:32.300
Have mercy on me.

1270
01:49:32.367 --> 01:49:35.367
My mercy brought us
to this cursed island.

1271
01:49:35.433 --> 01:49:36.833
I told them what was going to happen.

1272
01:49:36.900 --> 01:49:37.967
That's right.

1273
01:49:38.633 --> 01:49:41.567
And we chose to drown
rather than starve to death.

1274
01:49:47.233 --> 01:49:49.500
All right. Now's the time.

1275
01:49:49.567 --> 01:49:51.900
The wind has stopped! There's no wind anymore!

1276
01:49:55.833 --> 01:50:00.833
The lull lasted long enough
to lure us into Poseidon's sea.

1277
01:50:00.900 --> 01:50:02.300
Under the sky of Zeus.

1278
01:50:03.700 --> 01:50:06.167
The bow against the wind!

1279
01:50:06.233 --> 01:50:07.700
Port side! Port side!

1280
01:50:21.633 --> 01:50:23.100
Pray to the gods!

1281
01:50:24.433 --> 01:50:25.633
The bow against the wind!

1282
01:50:25.700 --> 01:50:27.367
Ask for forgiveness!

1283
01:50:27.433 --> 01:50:28.433
The wind!

1284
01:50:44.300 --> 01:50:46.100
The bow against the wind!

1285
01:50:46.967 --> 01:50:48.033
Against the Wind!

1286
01:50:50.967 --> 01:50:52.367
Odysseus!

1287
01:50:54.100 --> 01:50:55.700
The bow against the wind!

1288
01:51:09.167 --> 01:51:11.100
Surrender yourselves to the mercy of the gods!

1289
01:51:14.900 --> 01:51:17.100
Pray to Poseidon!

1290
01:51:23.833 --> 01:51:26.433
Ask for forgiveness!

1291
01:51:38.433 --> 01:51:39.433
Watch out!

1292
01:51:54.300 --> 01:51:57.167
The last thing I remember
was Zeus's heavy hand.

1293
01:52:09.633 --> 01:52:11.233
Eurylochus!

1294
01:52:42.100 --> 01:52:43.833
I don't know what happened to them.

1295
01:52:43.900 --> 01:52:44.900
Yes. you know.

1296
01:52:46.967 --> 01:52:48.767
What he told them would happen.

1297
01:52:49.300 --> 01:52:51.433
I was shipwrecked on your shore. all alone.

1298
01:52:51.500 --> 01:52:53.700
A Shipwreck on My Shore

1299
01:52:53.767 --> 01:52:56.233
with the wreckage of your boat and without a crew.

1300
01:52:58.233 --> 01:53:00.500
Half-dead. unconscious.

1301
01:53:23.100 --> 01:53:25.500
I saw you looking lost.

1302
01:53:25.567 --> 01:53:27.367
in a few days…

1303
01:53:27.433 --> 01:53:28.567
And then. weeks later.

1304
01:53:29.567 --> 01:53:31.767
I knew the lotus would
make your awakening easier.

1305
01:53:33.900 --> 01:53:36.633
So that the mind hurts less than the body.

1306
01:53:39.367 --> 01:53:40.567
And that's exactly what happened.

1307
01:53:43.033 --> 01:53:44.500
You liked it so much.

1308
01:53:45.900 --> 01:53:47.967
You did it with such enthusiasm.

1309
01:53:51.567 --> 01:53:53.100
And while I was taking care of you.

1310
01:53:53.700 --> 01:53:54.967
Thank you.

1311
01:53:55.033 --> 01:53:56.833
I started to love you.

1312
01:54:01.767 --> 01:54:03.433
How long have I been here?

1313
01:54:05.700 --> 01:54:08.300
I tell myself that I shouldn't
give it to you anymore.

1314
01:54:08.367 --> 01:54:10.367
that you could already remember.

1315
01:54:10.433 --> 01:54:12.633
How much?
- He was… so happy.

1316
01:54:12.700 --> 01:54:13.900
You were happy.

1317
01:54:13.967 --> 01:54:16.367
We were so happy. 
For years.

1318
01:54:16.433 --> 01:54:17.433
Years?

1319
01:54:24.367 --> 01:54:25.367
Seven years.

1320
01:54:26.433 --> 01:54:30.033
You gave me the lotus and held me captive.

1321
01:54:30.100 --> 01:54:31.433
I saved your life.

1322
01:54:32.567 --> 01:54:33.633
I restored your body.

1323
01:54:33.700 --> 01:54:36.700
But I didn't dare to restore your sanity.

1324
01:54:38.100 --> 01:54:39.367
He was happy.

1325
01:54:40.367 --> 01:54:43.633
Your heart has always been somewhere else.

1326
01:54:45.233 --> 01:54:48.100
And your mind would have followed suit
sooner or later.

1327
01:54:49.100 --> 01:54:50.500
How could you?

1328
01:54:50.567 --> 01:54:53.700
The lotus clouds 
the mind and saps the will…

1329
01:54:53.767 --> 01:54:56.433
But it doesn't change you.

1330
01:54:56.500 --> 01:54:59.433
For some reason. you
weren't ready to come back.

1331
01:54:59.500 --> 01:55:01.033
And now you are.

1332
01:55:02.500 --> 01:55:04.433
And I always knew it would be that way.

1333
01:55:04.500 --> 01:55:05.967
Why?

1334
01:55:06.033 --> 01:55:08.833
You were clutching something 
when I found you.

1335
01:55:08.900 --> 01:55:10.700
With a fist 
clenched for weeks on end.

1336
01:55:11.567 --> 01:55:13.033
He was begging.

1337
01:55:13.567 --> 01:55:14.900
Often in Athens.

1338
01:55:14.967 --> 01:55:17.767
But sometimes you would say another name.

1339
01:55:31.833 --> 01:55:32.833
Penelope.

1340
01:55:37.233 --> 01:55:38.767
Help me get back.

1341
01:55:39.833 --> 01:55:41.233
That's what I do.

1342
01:55:41.767 --> 01:55:44.100
You must take control of your destiny.

1343
01:55:45.833 --> 01:55:47.433
But you won't be able to control that.

1344
01:55:50.567 --> 01:55:52.567
He was left at the mercy of the storm.

1345
01:55:53.500 --> 01:55:55.167
He was left at the mercy of Poseidon.

1346
01:55:56.500 --> 01:55:58.100
And accept your punishment.

1347
01:56:00.767 --> 01:56:02.367
Zeus won't let them kill you.

1348
01:56:06.033 --> 01:56:09.433
Give up the struggle. 
give up control. and live.

1349
01:56:12.233 --> 01:56:16.033
This is a leap of faith that
you've never taken before. Odysseus.

1350
01:57:08.167 --> 01:57:09.167
Odysseus.

1351
01:57:17.300 --> 01:57:18.367
Where am I?

1352
01:57:20.500 --> 01:57:22.633
Don't you recognize your own home?

1353
01:58:01.767 --> 01:58:03.433
Zeus welcomes you. strangers.

1354
01:58:04.700 --> 01:58:05.700
How many of you are there?

1355
01:58:06.233 --> 01:58:07.233
Half a dozen.

1356
01:58:07.833 --> 01:58:09.167
Do you need something to eat?

1357
01:58:09.233 --> 01:58:10.900
And provisions.

1358
01:58:10.967 --> 01:58:12.433
For the road.

1359
01:58:12.500 --> 01:58:13.700
Where are you going?

1360
01:58:13.767 --> 01:58:15.100
We need pigs.

1361
01:58:15.167 --> 01:58:17.033
We're going to Pylos.

1362
01:58:17.100 --> 01:58:18.433
Are you pilgrims?

1363
01:58:19.300 --> 01:58:21.033
Do we look like pilgrims?

1364
01:58:21.100 --> 01:58:22.500
I can't see very well anymore.

1365
01:58:23.433 --> 01:58:25.100
By law. I am offering you food.

1366
01:58:25.167 --> 01:58:27.233
But I can't sell the pigs.

1367
01:58:27.300 --> 01:58:28.767
Don't worry.

1368
01:58:28.833 --> 01:58:29.833
We won't pay.

1369
01:58:33.567 --> 01:58:35.833
Please give my regards to your mother.

1370
01:58:38.900 --> 01:58:40.433
Say. "Please forgive me."

1371
01:58:40.500 --> 01:58:41.900
About Troy

1372
01:58:41.967 --> 01:58:44.300
and for everything that 
happened in my name.

1373
01:58:50.833 --> 01:58:53.033
I'm sad that I haven't 
heard from your father.

1374
01:58:53.100 --> 01:58:54.633
No. You knew.

1375
01:58:54.700 --> 01:58:57.100
It's not about where it is. but who it is.

1376
01:58:57.967 --> 01:59:00.567
And now. more than ever.
I hope to meet him.

1377
01:59:05.767 --> 01:59:07.900
Where are your dogs. old man?

1378
01:59:12.300 --> 01:59:14.100
I burn their bodies.

1379
01:59:18.767 --> 01:59:21.033
Can Zeus
offer you some food. traveler?

1380
01:59:22.567 --> 01:59:24.100
Were they attacked?

1381
01:59:24.167 --> 01:59:25.967
The dogs saved your servants.

1382
01:59:26.033 --> 01:59:28.167
No. first we burned their brothers.

1383
01:59:31.367 --> 01:59:33.233
What happened to Ithaca?

1384
01:59:33.300 --> 01:59:34.567
The Darkness.

1385
01:59:34.633 --> 01:59:37.900
Odysseus's law has been violated.

1386
01:59:37.967 --> 01:59:41.367
A kingdom without a king ever since
my master died.

1387
01:59:41.433 --> 01:59:42.500
Your master.

1388
01:59:43.167 --> 01:59:45.300
Odysseus.

1389
01:59:45.367 --> 01:59:46.900
A hero of the Trojan War.

1390
01:59:49.300 --> 01:59:50.833
But Odysseus is alive.

1391
01:59:50.900 --> 01:59:52.900
Beggars with false hopes!

1392
01:59:52.967 --> 01:59:55.033
Not anymore! Not anymore!

1393
01:59:55.100 --> 01:59:57.500
Not anymore! Not anymore.

1394
01:59:57.567 --> 01:59:59.300
Not anymore. Not anymore.

1395
01:59:59.367 --> 02:00:01.367
Telemachus. Telemachus…

1396
02:00:02.500 --> 02:00:03.433
Telemachus.

1397
02:00:03.500 --> 02:00:06.900
He's in danger. 
And I can't help him.

1398
02:00:06.967 --> 02:00:10.433
There must be others in Ithaca
who can help Odysseus's son.

1399
02:00:10.500 --> 02:00:13.367
The grooms control the palace.

1400
02:00:13.433 --> 02:00:16.033
No one is 
loyal to Odysseus anymore.

1401
02:00:17.233 --> 02:00:18.567
And what about the queen?

1402
02:00:19.233 --> 02:00:22.300
The beautiful. 
wise Penelope.

1403
02:00:22.367 --> 02:00:24.833
A hostage of the worst men.

1404
02:00:24.900 --> 02:00:26.833
Who will force her 
to get married.

1405
02:00:26.900 --> 02:00:29.367
What will she do 
even without her son?

1406
02:00:29.433 --> 02:00:30.700
Here. take it. Drink up.

1407
02:00:35.767 --> 02:00:37.300
What danger 
is Telemachus facing?

1408
02:00:38.233 --> 02:00:40.367
He's from Sparta.

1409
02:00:41.567 --> 02:00:46.100
His admirers sent thugs
to ambush him in the temple.

1410
02:00:47.033 --> 02:00:48.567
Give me some food for the road.

1411
02:00:49.100 --> 02:00:52.100
You'll never 
catch up to the bandits.

1412
02:00:52.167 --> 02:00:55.767
If I get to Pylos before
Telemachus. I can warn him.

1413
02:00:55.833 --> 02:00:57.367
Why are you helping?

1414
02:00:58.167 --> 02:01:00.033
If I tell you. don't get mad.

1415
02:01:00.700 --> 02:01:02.500
I would have fought alongside Odysseus at Troy.

1416
02:01:02.567 --> 02:01:03.567
Really?

1417
02:01:04.833 --> 02:01:06.500
He was a strict master.

1418
02:01:06.567 --> 02:01:07.700
But fair.

1419
02:01:09.167 --> 02:01:11.567
It was fair. and it was worth 
following it.

1420
02:01:12.500 --> 02:01:13.500
Did you love him?

1421
02:01:14.500 --> 02:01:16.367
Like his own son.

1422
02:01:16.433 --> 02:01:19.033
Well. he's not dead.

1423
02:01:20.167 --> 02:01:22.033
Is he coming home?

1424
02:01:22.100 --> 02:01:23.500
Soon.

1425
02:01:23.567 --> 02:01:25.500
To get his revenge.

1426
02:01:27.367 --> 02:01:28.767
Let them pay for everything.

1427
02:01:38.100 --> 02:01:40.967
I'll do the talking. 
They don't need to know who you are.

1428
02:01:41.033 --> 02:01:43.033
We shouldn't have hired security.

1429
02:01:43.700 --> 02:01:45.833
It will help 
if there are any problems.

1430
02:01:57.367 --> 02:01:58.500
Get moving!

1431
02:01:58.567 --> 02:02:00.567
I have a message. 
Please wait.

1432
02:02:02.233 --> 02:02:03.367
Evmei sent me!

1433
02:02:05.567 --> 02:02:06.567
Bring the beggar here.

1434
02:02:23.033 --> 02:02:25.500
Your people are staying here. 
And the weapons. too.

1435
02:02:42.633 --> 02:02:44.633
Zeus welcomes you.

1436
02:02:44.700 --> 02:02:46.500
Are you coming from far away?

1437
02:02:46.567 --> 02:02:47.700
Not quite.

1438
02:02:48.900 --> 02:02:50.833
Come stay the night.

1439
02:02:52.900 --> 02:02:55.233
We are coming to offer a sacrifice to Athena.

1440
02:02:57.367 --> 02:03:00.300
And the hospitality you
show will be appreciated.

1441
02:03:00.367 --> 02:03:02.567
Of course. 
Get rid of that beggar.

1442
02:03:02.633 --> 02:03:04.367
He brought me a message.

1443
02:03:05.367 --> 02:03:06.567
Did Eumaius send you?

1444
02:03:07.767 --> 02:03:08.900
Yes. sir.

1445
02:03:14.633 --> 02:03:15.967
All right. tell me.

1446
02:03:17.367 --> 02:03:18.900
It's personal.

1447
02:03:18.967 --> 02:03:19.967
They are…

1448
02:03:21.433 --> 02:03:22.500
They are priests.

1449
02:03:40.100 --> 02:03:41.700
It's time for you to go. old man.

1450
02:03:43.033 --> 02:03:44.033
Go on. stranger.

1451
02:03:46.900 --> 02:03:49.500
I've already spared the life of
an old man this week.

1452
02:03:49.567 --> 02:03:50.767
But he was blind.

1453
02:03:50.833 --> 02:03:51.833
You won't...

1454
02:04:31.100 --> 02:04:32.433
It's not bad for a beggar.

1455
02:04:33.167 --> 02:04:35.167
No one is born a beggar.

1456
02:04:35.233 --> 02:04:36.433
Pick up the clothes

1457
02:04:37.033 --> 02:04:38.433
and put them on over the armor.

1458
02:04:40.433 --> 02:04:41.700
Help me with his body.

1459
02:04:41.767 --> 02:04:43.100
We have to leave it here.

1460
02:04:43.167 --> 02:04:45.167
No.
- We have to run away.

1461
02:04:45.767 --> 02:04:47.033
That's not how we honor him.

1462
02:04:47.100 --> 02:04:48.567
That's how you survive.

1463
02:04:48.633 --> 02:04:50.033
He was my teacher.

1464
02:04:51.233 --> 02:04:52.567
It may have been Athens. 
as predicted.

1465
02:04:52.633 --> 02:04:54.100
His name was Mentor.

1466
02:04:54.633 --> 02:04:55.967
And now that we're safe.

1467
02:04:56.033 --> 02:04:58.033
We'll go to the unknown West

1468
02:04:58.100 --> 02:04:59.567
and we'll honor him.

1469
02:04:59.633 --> 02:05:01.767
The grooms sent them off.

1470
02:05:01.833 --> 02:05:03.900
More are on the way. 
We have to go.

1471
02:05:03.967 --> 02:05:05.033
Overnight?

1472
02:05:05.100 --> 02:05:06.367
It's better than staying.

1473
02:05:06.433 --> 02:05:09.100
Mentor remains in the heart
of Athens' oldest temple.

1474
02:05:09.633 --> 02:05:11.833
For now. we'll start it with fire.

1475
02:05:21.567 --> 02:05:22.833
Fire!

1476
02:05:27.167 --> 02:05:29.100
Fire! Fire!

1477
02:05:29.167 --> 02:05:30.833
Fire! Fire!

1478
02:05:50.767 --> 02:05:52.700
How is it that a beggar knows Mentor?

1479
02:05:52.767 --> 02:05:55.500
I already told you. 
I haven't always been a beggar.

1480
02:05:55.567 --> 02:05:57.633
You have wise eyes.

1481
02:05:57.700 --> 02:06:00.233
The Eyes of Athens.
- Do not look for gods in people.

1482
02:06:00.300 --> 02:06:02.033
You're going to be disappointed.

1483
02:06:02.100 --> 02:06:03.700
Then tell me who you are.

1484
02:06:05.433 --> 02:06:06.633
My name is Sinon.

1485
02:06:07.500 --> 02:06:10.433
I am a soldier who fought
under your father's command at Troy.

1486
02:06:11.633 --> 02:06:13.100
Did you know my father?

1487
02:06:13.167 --> 02:06:15.967
I've been following his orders for as long as I can remember.

1488
02:06:17.300 --> 02:06:18.567
What? Is he alive?

1489
02:06:19.633 --> 02:06:20.633
He is alive.

1490
02:06:22.233 --> 02:06:23.767
And where is he?

1491
02:06:23.833 --> 02:06:25.433
Nearby.

1492
02:06:25.500 --> 02:06:27.900
He sent me to assess the situation.

1493
02:06:29.567 --> 02:06:31.233
My mom won't believe it.

1494
02:06:31.300 --> 02:06:32.633
No. you shouldn't tell her.

1495
02:06:33.900 --> 02:06:34.967
What do you mean. nothing?

1496
02:06:36.167 --> 02:06:37.167
Tell her…

1497
02:06:38.300 --> 02:06:40.700
Tell her that you've decided she
needs to get married now.

1498
02:06:42.567 --> 02:06:43.833
There's no need to test her.

1499
02:06:43.900 --> 02:06:45.100
How could anyone doubt her?

1500
02:06:45.167 --> 02:06:46.633
He has no doubt.

1501
02:06:46.700 --> 02:06:48.967
She would have been justified in forgetting him.

1502
02:06:50.900 --> 02:06:52.567
Are there any faithful servants left?

1503
02:06:53.567 --> 02:06:54.567
Someone.

1504
02:06:55.433 --> 02:06:57.033
And when Odysseus arrived—everyone.

1505
02:06:59.033 --> 02:07:01.033
We need to gather 
the suitors at the palace.

1506
02:07:01.900 --> 02:07:03.233
That's never a problem.

1507
02:07:03.900 --> 02:07:05.833
Do you have any gifts from Menelaus?

1508
02:07:05.900 --> 02:07:06.900
Yes. on my ship.

1509
02:07:07.500 --> 02:07:08.833
Very good.

1510
02:07:08.900 --> 02:07:10.500
Come back as a prince.

1511
02:07:10.567 --> 02:07:12.300
Throw a party.

1512
02:07:12.367 --> 02:07:13.367
I'll be there.

1513
02:07:14.367 --> 02:07:15.967
What are you going to do at the party?

1514
02:07:16.567 --> 02:07:18.033
I'm going to beg.

1515
02:07:18.100 --> 02:07:20.300
You can see people better from below.

1516
02:07:22.100 --> 02:07:23.500
How many are there?

1517
02:07:24.367 --> 02:07:25.700
Tens.

1518
02:07:25.767 --> 02:07:27.500
But if his people are like you…

1519
02:07:27.567 --> 02:07:28.900
There's no one here.

1520
02:07:29.900 --> 02:07:30.900
What? Just you?

1521
02:07:32.300 --> 02:07:33.433
You. too.

1522
02:07:39.300 --> 02:07:40.567
Telemachus has returned.

1523
02:07:40.633 --> 02:07:42.167
What?
- Telemachus has returned.

1524
02:07:43.233 --> 02:07:45.833
Who says so?
- Everyone.

1525
02:07:45.900 --> 02:07:46.900
Where is Telemachus?

1526
02:07:47.767 --> 02:07:50.567
My queen! 
But isn't your place up there?

1527
02:07:58.433 --> 02:07:59.767
Is that true?

1528
02:08:02.500 --> 02:08:03.500
He came back.

1529
02:08:04.567 --> 02:08:05.967
I'm back.

1530
02:08:06.033 --> 02:08:07.700
I was thinking we could celebrate today.

1531
02:08:08.567 --> 02:08:11.567
If there's anything left at all.

1532
02:08:13.100 --> 02:08:14.433
Don't worry.

1533
02:08:14.500 --> 02:08:17.367
My suitors will give us
gifts to celebrate your return.

1534
02:08:17.433 --> 02:08:19.500
I'm glad to see you safe and sound.

1535
02:08:30.367 --> 02:08:31.567
I'm glad to see you back on your feet.

1536
02:08:32.567 --> 02:08:34.367
It was high time 
I took you to the palace.

1537
02:08:38.033 --> 02:08:39.300
What did you learn?

1538
02:08:41.500 --> 02:08:42.500
Nothing.

1539
02:08:43.167 --> 02:08:46.767
Stories about the war. but nothing
about what happened after they left Troy.

1540
02:08:47.433 --> 02:08:48.567
I'm sorry. Mom.

1541
02:08:49.500 --> 02:08:53.233
Everyone in Sparta is talking
about the impending disaster.

1542
02:08:54.033 --> 02:08:56.167
An age of darkness. 
because the seafaring peoples

1543
02:08:56.233 --> 02:08:57.900
violate the Law of Zeus.

1544
02:08:57.967 --> 02:08:59.433
So it's true.

1545
02:08:59.500 --> 02:09:01.833
Our civilization is collapsing.

1546
02:09:01.900 --> 02:09:03.567
Menelaus says 
that we won't be able to raise an army

1547
02:09:03.633 --> 02:09:05.100
with the throne vacant.

1548
02:09:05.167 --> 02:09:06.500
We made a plan.

1549
02:09:08.033 --> 02:09:09.100
What for?

1550
02:09:11.300 --> 02:09:12.733
So that the suitors would leave.

1551
02:09:15.567 --> 02:09:17.167
It's time to choose.

1552
02:09:20.967 --> 02:09:22.633
Do you want me to get married?

1553
02:09:22.700 --> 02:09:25.633
If you do that. 
the others will leave.

1554
02:09:25.700 --> 02:09:27.300
So I'll just deal with one.

1555
02:09:27.367 --> 02:09:28.500
I'm almost an adult.

1556
02:09:28.567 --> 02:09:32.500
I will tell the elders that
I learned of Odysseus's death in Sparta.

1557
02:09:32.567 --> 02:09:35.567
Menelaus will support me.
and they will let me take the throne.

1558
02:09:35.633 --> 02:09:36.433
Your father and I left home.

1559
02:09:36.500 --> 02:09:37.967
To renounce.

1560
02:09:38.033 --> 02:09:39.233
To live with a snake.

1561
02:09:39.300 --> 02:09:41.300
Is that your plan? Do you have a better one?

1562
02:09:44.633 --> 02:09:47.300
Burn down the palace so that they will burn

1563
02:09:47.367 --> 02:09:48.367
the girls.

1564
02:09:48.967 --> 02:09:49.767
Do you think that if I get married again.

1565
02:09:49.833 --> 02:09:52.367
Will everything go back to normal?

1566
02:09:53.633 --> 02:09:55.300
This world has come to an end.

1567
02:09:56.967 --> 02:09:59.333
And in the new one. they won't give power
to either you or Odysseus.

1568
02:10:01.300 --> 02:10:02.633
If he comes back. 
he'll have to kill them.

1569
02:10:02.700 --> 02:10:03.967
Just as you should do.

1570
02:10:04.567 --> 02:10:07.167
If I kill them. their
families will seek revenge.

1571
02:10:07.700 --> 02:10:08.867
They would have sent me into exile.

1572
02:10:09.833 --> 02:10:11.567
And without me. there is no throne.

1573
02:10:11.633 --> 02:10:13.567
Because only a man 
can sit on it. right?

1574
02:10:15.233 --> 02:10:16.233
An empty throne?

1575
02:10:18.033 --> 02:10:21.567
I've been sitting there for twenty years!

1576
02:10:21.633 --> 02:10:24.433
My knowledge. 
my many years of experience

1577
02:10:24.500 --> 02:10:27.167
are nothing compared to
your barely sprouting mustache.

1578
02:10:29.900 --> 02:10:32.300
Do you want peace?

1579
02:10:32.367 --> 02:10:34.100
Do you want revenge?
- I want Odysseus!

1580
02:10:37.633 --> 02:10:38.633
Hey…

1581
02:10:39.433 --> 02:10:40.767
Muzo. for one person

1582
02:10:43.833 --> 02:10:44.833
one person

1583
02:10:49.767 --> 02:10:50.833
a fleet

1584
02:10:55.167 --> 02:10:56.167
a war

1585
02:11:00.567 --> 02:11:01.700
in Troy…

1586
02:11:05.767 --> 02:11:07.633
Sing. Muso. about a man

1587
02:11:11.967 --> 02:11:13.100
a man

1588
02:11:17.300 --> 02:11:18.367
an idea

1589
02:11:22.300 --> 02:11:23.633
A little trick…

1590
02:11:27.300 --> 02:11:28.433
Go ahead. Eumaios.

1591
02:11:35.700 --> 02:11:36.833
Argos.

1592
02:11:59.433 --> 02:12:01.300
Bury him there. on the rock.

1593
02:12:03.367 --> 02:12:04.567
Where did you meet?

1594
02:12:13.367 --> 02:12:14.633
You are Odysseus.

1595
02:12:17.833 --> 02:12:19.367
You're my father.

1596
02:12:21.833 --> 02:12:22.900
I'm a beggar.

1597
02:12:26.433 --> 02:12:27.433
And you

1598
02:12:29.633 --> 02:12:30.900
you are the worthy one.

1599
02:12:32.300 --> 02:12:35.100
Odysseus's handsome son.

1600
02:12:36.900 --> 02:12:40.233
With whom. when they finally meet.

1601
02:12:42.300 --> 02:12:43.900
He'll be so proud.

1602
02:12:47.767 --> 02:12:50.633
Welcome home… stranger.

1603
02:12:53.900 --> 02:12:55.900
The Darkness of Victory

1604
02:12:56.700 --> 02:12:58.567
envelops the creators of legends

1605
02:13:00.900 --> 02:13:03.367
and turns us into blind bards and beggars.

1606
02:13:10.900 --> 02:13:12.967
Accept Zeus' hospitality.

1607
02:13:13.567 --> 02:13:15.300
Feed the stranger.

1608
02:13:15.367 --> 02:13:17.167
No. sir.

1609
02:13:17.233 --> 02:13:19.233
He'll just walk by with his bowl.

1610
02:13:24.633 --> 02:13:27.300
Did you really have to bring yet another
sleazy sycophant to dinner?

1611
02:13:27.367 --> 02:13:29.300
So what. 
just another beggar at the feast?

1612
02:13:47.433 --> 02:13:50.033
We have to kill him before he
regains his influence among the elders.

1613
02:13:50.100 --> 02:13:51.367
How?

1614
02:14:06.233 --> 02:14:08.033
Bring some wine!

1615
02:14:08.100 --> 02:14:09.700
Challenge him.

1616
02:14:09.767 --> 02:14:11.233
Let him grab a weapon.

1617
02:14:15.767 --> 02:14:18.633
This isn't the pallet 
we were messing with.

1618
02:14:18.700 --> 02:14:21.100
Then we'll go back there
tonight and slit his throat.

1619
02:14:22.833 --> 02:14:24.367
Get lost. old man.
- Please.

1620
02:14:25.100 --> 02:14:26.167
I haven't eaten in days.

1621
02:14:26.233 --> 02:14:27.367
Get lost!

1622
02:14:27.433 --> 02:14:30.700
Can't you be generous
with food that isn't yours?

1623
02:14:32.767 --> 02:14:34.300
There's no room in your cup.

1624
02:14:34.367 --> 02:14:35.633
Yes. there is.

1625
02:14:40.833 --> 02:14:41.833
Well. there was.

1626
02:14:43.900 --> 02:14:44.900
Take it.

1627
02:14:49.233 --> 02:14:51.033
You must be expecting something from me.

1628
02:14:51.100 --> 02:14:52.567
No.

1629
02:14:52.633 --> 02:14:54.567
I have something for you.

1630
02:14:55.367 --> 02:14:56.700
Do you have something for me?

1631
02:14:57.833 --> 02:14:59.367
There's something for me!

1632
02:15:01.167 --> 02:15:04.700
A filthy sycophant like you. what

1633
02:15:04.767 --> 02:15:07.700
Is there anything there that I might need?

1634
02:15:07.767 --> 02:15:10.500
Actually. it's yours.

1635
02:15:15.700 --> 02:15:17.633
They asked me to give it back to you.

1636
02:15:19.700 --> 02:15:20.700
It's not mine.

1637
02:15:26.900 --> 02:15:28.300
Where did you get that?

1638
02:15:30.567 --> 02:15:32.567
Don't turn your back on me!

1639
02:15:37.700 --> 02:15:40.967
Antinous. you can't
treat a beggar like that.

1640
02:15:41.033 --> 02:15:42.233
This is the Law of Zeus.

1641
02:15:42.300 --> 02:15:43.900
The Law of Zeus.

1642
02:15:48.433 --> 02:15:49.767
Do you think he's a god?

1643
02:15:53.300 --> 02:15:55.167
Is God predetermined?

1644
02:15:56.033 --> 02:15:57.167
Is that who you are?

1645
02:15:58.833 --> 02:16:00.167
Are you God.

1646
02:16:01.233 --> 02:16:02.233
Is that a plate or a bowl?

1647
02:16:05.167 --> 02:16:07.100
I am a veteran of the Trojan War.

1648
02:16:07.700 --> 02:16:09.700
A veteran of Troy.

1649
02:16:12.500 --> 02:16:13.767
And what's your name?

1650
02:16:18.367 --> 02:16:19.367
Synonym.

1651
02:16:22.900 --> 02:16:24.700
Does that name ring a bell?

1652
02:16:28.100 --> 02:16:31.900
I wear it in honor of the bravest
young man I ever knew.

1653
02:16:32.500 --> 02:16:33.500
Synonym.

1654
02:16:35.100 --> 02:16:36.967
Wasn't he your shepherd's son?

1655
02:16:39.300 --> 02:16:40.433
I don't remember.

1656
02:16:40.500 --> 02:16:43.567
Yeah. yeah. You wanted to go in his place.

1657
02:16:43.633 --> 02:16:45.367
Where did you find him?

1658
02:16:45.433 --> 02:16:47.433
Where you'll be soon.

1659
02:16:47.500 --> 02:16:49.033
And where is that?

1660
02:16:49.900 --> 02:16:50.900
Hades.

1661
02:16:54.367 --> 02:16:55.700
Enough!

1662
02:16:57.167 --> 02:16:59.033
The feast is over!

1663
02:17:03.033 --> 02:17:04.033
Let's go.

1664
02:17:05.500 --> 02:17:06.767
That's not mine!

1665
02:17:15.367 --> 02:17:17.567
The Queen would like to speak with you.

1666
02:17:17.633 --> 02:17:20.033
Wait until they leave 
and light the fire.

1667
02:18:10.833 --> 02:18:12.233
Hello. traveler.

1668
02:18:13.233 --> 02:18:14.633
My queen.

1669
02:18:14.700 --> 02:18:18.500
Melanto. let Eurycleia
wash the stranger's feet.

1670
02:18:18.567 --> 02:18:20.033
There's no need.

1671
02:18:20.100 --> 02:18:24.233
My husband insisted that
we treat our guests as if they were family.

1672
02:18:24.300 --> 02:18:26.500
Do you know anything about Odysseus. 
my husband?

1673
02:18:28.233 --> 02:18:29.500
From the songs.

1674
02:18:30.100 --> 02:18:32.167
I was in Troy. ma'am.

1675
02:18:32.233 --> 02:18:34.433
You probably think these songs 
are silly.

1676
02:18:35.767 --> 02:18:37.367
They make me cry.

1677
02:18:38.300 --> 02:18:39.700
Why?

1678
02:18:39.767 --> 02:18:41.500
Because of what was lost.

1679
02:18:42.300 --> 02:18:43.500
Are they alive?

1680
02:18:44.167 --> 02:18:46.567
And the years.

1681
02:18:46.633 --> 02:18:47.700
Everything.

1682
02:18:55.233 --> 02:18:56.967
Did Odysseus meet her in Troy?

1683
02:18:59.767 --> 02:19:03.900
Did you notice any details about
his clothes. anything he was wearing?

1684
02:19:04.500 --> 02:19:06.700
She was wearing a strange brooch.

1685
02:19:06.767 --> 02:19:08.567
Weird?

1686
02:19:08.633 --> 02:19:09.700
Beautiful.

1687
02:19:10.500 --> 02:19:11.700
But that's beside the point.

1688
02:19:12.300 --> 02:19:13.300
Describe her.

1689
02:19:27.233 --> 02:19:28.700
I'm going to get some more water.

1690
02:19:33.567 --> 02:19:36.233
It was a beautiful gold brooch.

1691
02:19:37.633 --> 02:19:40.567
With the image of Athena. 
surrounded by vines.

1692
02:19:49.100 --> 02:19:50.633
Did you wear it often?

1693
02:19:52.100 --> 02:19:53.433
You never took it off.

1694
02:20:13.633 --> 02:20:16.567
How come he returned home. 
but Odysseus didn't?

1695
02:20:17.633 --> 02:20:20.433
I'm not home. Not yet. anyway.

1696
02:20:21.233 --> 02:20:22.567
Why not?

1697
02:20:22.633 --> 02:20:25.167
It's not easy to come back home.

1698
02:20:26.033 --> 02:20:28.700
That's true for Odysseus and for me.

1699
02:20:29.833 --> 02:20:32.567
Odysseus. as I knew him.

1700
02:20:32.633 --> 02:20:37.367
He would do even the impossible to
come back to me. no matter what.

1701
02:20:38.233 --> 02:20:41.633
What if Odysseus. whom
you knew. had gone missing?

1702
02:20:46.233 --> 02:20:49.367
What if one night.

1703
02:20:50.633 --> 02:20:52.100
in an unfamiliar city.

1704
02:20:53.900 --> 02:20:55.233
He has seen things.

1705
02:20:56.633 --> 02:20:59.167
that would make him think
that the home he once knew.

1706
02:21:00.567 --> 02:21:02.567
Could it be that it's no longer there?

1707
02:21:08.100 --> 02:21:10.433
When it emerges from the horse's womb

1708
02:21:14.300 --> 02:21:16.100
and opened the gates of Troy.

1709
02:21:21.567 --> 02:21:24.033
He has seen ten years of wrath.

1710
02:21:25.033 --> 02:21:27.367
to pour down on the city overnight?

1711
02:21:51.633 --> 02:21:52.633
Seven.

1712
02:22:14.833 --> 02:22:17.100
Everyone. now!

1713
02:23:43.100 --> 02:23:46.233
We left them a gift.

1714
02:23:46.300 --> 02:23:48.833
A gift of peace.

1715
02:23:48.900 --> 02:23:51.633
The ones who took you into their home.

1716
02:24:02.233 --> 02:24:05.033
We trampled on every sacred bond

1717
02:24:06.500 --> 02:24:08.100
among nations.

1718
02:24:10.033 --> 02:24:11.100
We turned the battle around…

1719
02:24:12.033 --> 02:24:13.300
Destroy their idols!

1720
02:24:15.167 --> 02:24:16.500
On a hunting trip.

1721
02:24:20.633 --> 02:24:21.633
No. please.

1722
02:24:32.167 --> 02:24:34.900
To burn down the walls of Troy

1723
02:24:34.967 --> 02:24:37.233
...was to burn the whole world to ashes.

1724
02:24:38.967 --> 02:24:40.767
Including his own home.

1725
02:24:47.433 --> 02:24:49.633
What if he had known?

1726
02:24:50.300 --> 02:24:51.633
that night.

1727
02:24:54.900 --> 02:24:59.100
walking through the flames
of anarchy and pain

1728
02:25:48.433 --> 02:25:51.633
and in the days that followed. filled with
exhausting celebrations…

1729
02:25:58.033 --> 02:26:01.233
What if he knew exactly 
what he had done?

1730
02:26:19.900 --> 02:26:21.833
What all of you did.

1731
02:26:21.900 --> 02:26:23.633
One person's idea.

1732
02:26:25.167 --> 02:26:26.500
A man's cunning.

1733
02:26:28.300 --> 02:26:30.900
He broke the Law of Zeus forever.

1734
02:26:32.233 --> 02:26:35.233
We lived in a world of 
palaces and trade.

1735
02:26:37.233 --> 02:26:38.433
On the word.

1736
02:26:38.500 --> 02:26:39.900
Blind to its beauty.

1737
02:26:41.100 --> 02:26:42.767
Until we destroyed it.

1738
02:26:45.100 --> 02:26:47.433
Are you the maritime peoples?

1739
02:26:49.900 --> 02:26:51.100
Yes. my queen.

1740
02:26:52.833 --> 02:26:56.167
Violations of the Law of
Zeus are spreading like the plague.

1741
02:26:56.233 --> 02:26:58.033
Our Bronze Age is coming to an end.

1742
02:26:58.100 --> 02:27:01.767
And perhaps he couldn't bear to
see the ruins of what had been done.

1743
02:27:01.833 --> 02:27:02.900
Nowhere.

1744
02:27:03.900 --> 02:27:05.900
And especially in my own home.

1745
02:27:15.433 --> 02:27:16.767
But Odysseus is alive.

1746
02:27:19.167 --> 02:27:21.367
And it will come 
when you need it most.

1747
02:27:23.367 --> 02:27:24.633
Well…

1748
02:27:25.967 --> 02:27:28.767
I lived in its ruins for a very long time.

1749
02:27:34.367 --> 02:27:35.700
Bring the grooms at daybreak.

1750
02:27:35.767 --> 02:27:37.700
Remove the weapons from the room.

1751
02:27:37.767 --> 02:27:40.167
In a few hours. one of them will be happy. and

1752
02:27:40.233 --> 02:27:42.167
The rest will seek revenge.

1753
02:27:42.233 --> 02:27:44.967
The bronze is tempting. 
so no weapons.

1754
02:27:45.033 --> 02:27:46.500
And we'll close the doors.

1755
02:27:47.233 --> 02:27:49.633
I will marry the one who
passes my test.

1756
02:27:56.233 --> 02:27:57.967
How are we going to stand up to everyone?

1757
02:27:58.767 --> 02:28:00.633
Just me.

1758
02:28:00.700 --> 02:28:03.100
Their blood is on my hands.

1759
02:28:03.167 --> 02:28:04.433
Or they'll send you into exile.

1760
02:28:05.700 --> 02:28:06.700
Don't you have one?

1761
02:28:07.700 --> 02:28:10.033
Don't let them get their hands on the weapons.

1762
02:28:10.100 --> 02:28:12.033
I'll take care of it.

1763
02:28:12.100 --> 02:28:14.700
Even without weapons. there are far too many of them.

1764
02:28:14.767 --> 02:28:16.433
Besides. you won't be armed.

1765
02:28:17.900 --> 02:28:19.233
I'll have a weapon.

1766
02:28:20.967 --> 02:28:22.433
Your mom has already taken care of it.

1767
02:28:25.033 --> 02:28:26.833
Gather the trusted servants.

1768
02:28:26.900 --> 02:28:29.100
Put the weapons away in the upstairs storage room.

1769
02:28:29.167 --> 02:28:30.433
Yes. I'll take care of it.

1770
02:28:48.500 --> 02:28:50.500
Come on. come on. Put down your weapons.

1771
02:28:58.567 --> 02:28:59.633
Hurry. hurry.

1772
02:29:22.367 --> 02:29:24.367
For Zeus. the result is what matters.

1773
02:29:24.433 --> 02:29:25.833
But what is the test?

1774
02:29:50.167 --> 02:29:51.633
For years on end.

1775
02:29:52.633 --> 02:29:54.233
without my husband here.

1776
02:29:55.100 --> 02:29:57.500
They took over the palace for their feasts.

1777
02:29:58.567 --> 02:30:01.767
Their proposal was to win my hand in marriage.

1778
02:30:05.300 --> 02:30:09.500
And as I listened to their banter and their arguments.

1779
02:30:11.633 --> 02:30:15.967
They were corrupting my servants
and showing no respect for my home.

1780
02:30:16.033 --> 02:30:18.433
All I wanted was 
for Odysseus to return

1781
02:30:18.500 --> 02:30:20.300
and see everything with his own eyes.

1782
02:30:27.100 --> 02:30:30.300
But last night I realized.

1783
02:30:30.367 --> 02:30:32.233
that he isn't here right now.

1784
02:30:35.233 --> 02:30:36.567
That it will never happen.

1785
02:30:38.033 --> 02:30:39.033
That is why

1786
02:30:40.300 --> 02:30:43.767
I'm ready to marry anyone.

1787
02:30:43.833 --> 02:30:46.100
whoever manages to draw this bow

1788
02:30:46.167 --> 02:30:49.233
and shoot an arrow through
those twelve axes.

1789
02:30:50.767 --> 02:30:52.167
Go ahead.

1790
02:30:53.967 --> 02:30:55.433
And prove to me who you are.

1791
02:30:56.967 --> 02:30:58.033
Eumeus.

1792
02:31:09.700 --> 02:31:11.233
Who comes first?

1793
02:31:11.300 --> 02:31:12.433
Give me the bow.

1794
02:31:32.967 --> 02:31:34.167
It's impossible.

1795
02:31:34.233 --> 02:31:35.633
For you.

1796
02:31:35.700 --> 02:31:38.033
As a child. I saw Odysseus
do it with ease.

1797
02:31:38.100 --> 02:31:39.100
Go ahead and do it. then.

1798
02:31:39.967 --> 02:31:40.967
I'll wait my turn.

1799
02:31:42.167 --> 02:31:44.633
What if we heat and mash the onion?

1800
02:31:45.833 --> 02:31:47.967
It hasn't been stretched for years.

1801
02:32:21.233 --> 02:32:22.567
It's your turn.

1802
02:32:22.633 --> 02:32:23.833
There's no need.

1803
02:32:26.967 --> 02:32:28.767
The queen proved me right.

1804
02:32:28.833 --> 02:32:30.833
We can't replace Odysseus.

1805
02:32:30.900 --> 02:32:32.367
Some of us already knew that.

1806
02:32:32.433 --> 02:32:35.300
Some of us were going to follow
this great man to Troy.

1807
02:32:35.367 --> 02:32:36.567
If you had asked.

1808
02:32:39.233 --> 02:32:40.900
But he never did come back.

1809
02:32:42.767 --> 02:32:44.367
So choose the best one.

1810
02:32:46.100 --> 02:32:48.700
We failed your test.

1811
02:32:48.767 --> 02:32:50.100
I haven't failed.

1812
02:32:54.900 --> 02:32:56.833
Get those beggars out of here!

1813
02:32:57.633 --> 02:33:00.700
It is closed until the test is over.

1814
02:33:00.767 --> 02:33:02.100
The exam is over.

1815
02:33:03.367 --> 02:33:04.700
I want to give it a try.

1816
02:33:04.767 --> 02:33:06.633
Don't push it. old man.

1817
02:33:07.767 --> 02:33:09.700
No. he has the right to try.

1818
02:33:10.767 --> 02:33:13.167
If you don't want to. let him try.

1819
02:33:13.233 --> 02:33:15.500
You're making fun of 
your mother's wishes.

1820
02:33:15.567 --> 02:33:17.967
My mom wants all of 
you to burn.

1821
02:33:29.433 --> 02:33:31.767
He is a veteran marksman.

1822
02:33:35.300 --> 02:33:36.300
Hurry up.

1823
02:33:36.833 --> 02:33:38.900
Watch the expert in action.

1824
02:33:38.967 --> 02:33:39.967
Suck-up!

1825
02:33:41.500 --> 02:33:42.500
Hurry up!

1826
02:33:43.233 --> 02:33:44.633
Forget it. old man!

1827
02:33:45.700 --> 02:33:46.700
Hey. old man!

1828
02:33:47.433 --> 02:33:48.433
Hurry up!

1829
02:33:56.033 --> 02:33:57.767
All right. then. my friend.

1830
02:33:57.833 --> 02:33:59.233
That's enough. my friend.

1831
02:33:59.300 --> 02:34:01.233
Let him try. Let him try. Go ahead.

1832
02:34:43.767 --> 02:34:44.767
You're Odys…

1833
02:34:51.767 --> 02:34:53.767
But isn't that where you belong. down there?

1834
02:35:07.833 --> 02:35:08.833
Is that him?

1835
02:35:09.833 --> 02:35:11.033
He is.

1836
02:35:20.633 --> 02:35:21.767
Keep the weapons safe.

1837
02:35:36.233 --> 02:35:37.633
Where are the weapons?

1838
02:35:37.700 --> 02:35:38.967
In the warehouse upstairs.

1839
02:35:39.033 --> 02:35:40.033
Bring them here!

1840
02:35:44.167 --> 02:35:47.567
What are you waiting for? 
He's going to kill us all!

1841
02:35:51.167 --> 02:35:52.167
Be careful!

1842
02:35:57.233 --> 02:35:59.767
I told you. 
You're making a racket. you cowherd.

1843
02:35:59.833 --> 02:36:02.300
No! No! No!

1844
02:36:02.367 --> 02:36:05.167
Eumei! Let me out!

1845
02:36:10.500 --> 02:36:11.500
Down!

1846
02:36:22.500 --> 02:36:23.567
No!

1847
02:37:18.633 --> 02:37:20.033
The swords!

1848
02:38:20.167 --> 02:38:21.833
The shields! The shields!

1849
02:39:00.633 --> 02:39:01.633
Copies!

1850
02:39:32.033 --> 02:39:33.433
Use the copies!

1851
02:40:27.367 --> 02:40:28.833
Did you think you'd beat everyone?

1852
02:40:29.700 --> 02:40:31.167
It's enough for me to kill you.

1853
02:40:32.300 --> 02:40:33.567
Get it over with.

1854
02:40:36.633 --> 02:40:37.633
Dad!

1855
02:41:07.900 --> 02:41:08.967
When you arrive in Hades.

1856
02:41:11.433 --> 02:41:15.100
Tell my people that I'm going
west to pay my respects to them.

1857
02:41:21.100 --> 02:41:22.100
And tell Sinon

1858
02:41:24.900 --> 02:41:27.167
that I brought shame upon you.

1859
02:41:46.633 --> 02:41:47.700
What happened?

1860
02:41:49.233 --> 02:41:50.833
Did you beat them?

1861
02:41:51.633 --> 02:41:53.433
Is he dead?

1862
02:41:54.633 --> 02:41:57.367
We destroyed the fragile 
bond between people.

1863
02:42:06.100 --> 02:42:09.100
And in the centuries of darkness to come

1864
02:42:09.167 --> 02:42:12.900
The stories of Troy 
will be sung only…

1865
02:43:33.033 --> 02:43:35.367
Odysseus. my love.

1866
02:43:35.433 --> 02:43:36.433
Please. my love.

1867
02:43:38.233 --> 02:43:39.367
Odysseus.

1868
02:43:40.100 --> 02:43:42.367
My love. You're back.

1869
02:43:44.167 --> 02:43:45.900
Last but not least.

1870
02:43:45.967 --> 02:43:47.167
Finally.

1871
02:43:48.633 --> 02:43:52.567
To see my beloved
Ithaca. One last time.

1872
02:43:53.633 --> 02:43:55.233
No. No.

1873
02:43:55.300 --> 02:43:56.767
No. You can't. no.

1874
02:43:56.833 --> 02:43:58.033
You can't. Not right now.

1875
02:43:58.100 --> 02:43:59.833
Please. I've been waiting so long.

1876
02:44:02.167 --> 02:44:03.367
You can't die.

1877
02:44:04.633 --> 02:44:05.767
No. it's not death.

1878
02:44:07.833 --> 02:44:09.033
This is exile.

1879
02:44:16.467 --> 02:44:18.467
Telemachus will be king.

1880
02:44:22.000 --> 02:44:26.933
We'll head west. into the unknown.
to honor our people. together.

1881
02:44:29.733 --> 02:44:34.100
Build the fastest
ship. assemble the best

1882
02:44:34.167 --> 02:44:37.267
Gather your crew and set sail 
toward the horizon.

1883
02:44:37.333 --> 02:44:39.633
We will follow 
the setting sun.

1884
02:44:40.833 --> 02:44:41.800
A person…

1885
02:44:41.867 --> 02:44:44.867
Why are these stories 
just going to be glorified?

1886
02:44:46.767 --> 02:44:49.100
Because the songs will be
the only thing they'll have.

1887
02:44:49.167 --> 02:44:51.533
so that they may remember those
who knew how to write.

1888
02:44:52.967 --> 02:44:54.233
…a war…

1889
02:44:55.233 --> 02:44:57.833
Civilization will be reborn.

